window.CONCEPTS = window.CONCEPTS || {};
(function(){
const R = String.raw;

/* =====================================================================
   MIDTERM 1
   ===================================================================== */

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/convex"] = [
  R`<b>Open, closed, compact.</b> The open ball is $B(r,\epsilon)=\{r'\in\mathbb R^N:\|r-r'\|&lt;\epsilon\}$. A set $\mathcal A$ is <em>open</em> if every point has an open ball inside $\mathcal A$; <em>closed</em> if its complement is open; <em>compact</em> if closed and bounded. $\mathbb R^N$ is both open and closed but not compact. In a compact set every sequence has a limit point inside the set. (Week 3 slides)`,
  R`<b>Convex set.</b> $\mathcal A$ is convex if $\forall\lambda\in[0,1]$ and $\forall r,s\in\mathcal A$, $\lambda r+(1-\lambda)s\in\mathcal A$ (the segment joining any two points stays inside). The intersection of convex sets is convex. The simplex $\mathcal S_M$ and any half-space or ball are convex; an annulus or a set with a "dent" is not.`,
  R`<b>Convex function.</b> $f:\mathcal A\to\mathbb R$ on a convex set is convex if $f(\lambda r+(1-\lambda)s)\le\lambda f(r)+(1-\lambda)f(s)$ for all $\lambda\in[0,1]$: the chord is never below the graph. <em>Strictly</em> convex uses $&lt;$ for $r\ne s$, $\lambda\in(0,1)$. $f$ is <em>concave</em> if $-f$ is convex. An affine map $f(y)=Ay+b$ is both convex and concave (but neither strictly).`,
  R`<b>Operations that preserve convexity.</b> Sums of convex functions are convex; the pointwise maximum $f(x)=\max_i f_i(x)$ of convex functions is convex; a convex function of a linear (affine) transform $f(Ay)$ is convex; nonnegative scaling. For $C^2$ functions: $f$ is convex $\iff$ the Hessian $H_{i,j}=\partial^2f/\partial y_i\partial y_j$ is non-negative definite everywhere; $H\succ0$ everywhere suffices for strict convexity.`,
  R`<b>Pitfalls on the check-box questions.</b> Products of convex functions need not be convex ($x\cdot x^2=x^3$); compositions $f(g(x))$ need $f$ convex <em>and non-decreasing</em> with $g$ convex; a convex function need not be differentiable ($|x|$); a convex function need not have a minimum ($e^{-x}$, $Ay+b$); a strictly convex function need not have a minimum ($e^{x}$).`,
  R`<b>Local minimum, local maximum, saddle.</b> $r_0$ is a local minimum if $\exists\epsilon>0$ with $f(r)\ge f(r_0)$ for all $r\in\mathcal A\cap B(r_0,\epsilon)$ (reverse the inequality for a local maximum). Necessary condition: if $f$ is continuously differentiable then $\nabla f(r_0)=0$ at any interior local extremum. A <em>saddle point</em> has $\nabla f(r_0)=0$ but is neither a local min nor max, e.g. $f(x_1,x_2)=x_1^2-x_2^2$ at the origin (Hessian $\mathrm{diag}(2,-2)$, eigenvalues of both signs) or the 1-D inflection $x^3$ at 0.`,
  R`<b>Zero gradient is necessary, not sufficient.</b> $\nabla f=0$ can be a min, a max, an inflection ($x^3$) or a saddle ($x^2-y^2$, $xy$ with Hessian eigenvalues $\pm1$). To classify a stationary point look at the Hessian: $\succ0$ local min, $\prec0$ local max, indefinite saddle, singular undecided.`,
  R`<b>Global minimum.</b> $r_0$ is a global minimum if $f(r)\ge f(r_0)$ for all $r\in\mathcal A$. In general it is hard to find; gradient descent typically becomes trapped in local minima and most interesting functions in ML are <em>not</em> convex. (Week 3 slides)`,
  R`<b>The three optimization theorems.</b> (1) If $f$ is continuous and $\mathcal A$ is compact then $f$ attains a global minimum on $\mathcal A$ (e.g. any continuous $f$ on $[0,1]^N$). (2) If $f$ is convex on $\mathcal A$ then every local minimum is a global minimum. (3) If $f$ is continuously differentiable and convex then $\nabla f(r^\ast)=0$ implies $r^\ast$ is a global minimum. Consequence: GD converges to the global minimum of $C^1$ convex functions.`,
  R`<b>Proof sketch: convex local min is global.</b> Suppose $r_0$ is a local min but $f(s)&lt;f(r_0)$. For $\lambda\in(0,1]$, $f(\lambda s+(1-\lambda)r_0)\le\lambda f(s)+(1-\lambda)f(r_0)&lt;f(r_0)$, and as $\lambda\to0$ these points enter every ball $B(r_0,\epsilon)$, contradicting local minimality. <b>Uniqueness for strictly convex:</b> two minimizers $r\ne s$ with $f(r)=f(s)=m$ give $f(\tfrac{r+s}2)&lt;m$, contradiction. Convex (not strict) minimizers need not be unique: a constant function, or $\max(0,|x|-1)$.`,
  R`<b>Important facts to quote.</b> A global minimum may not be unique. If $\mathcal A$ is closed but not bounded, $f$ may not attain a minimum ($e^{-x}$ on $\mathbb R$). A continuous function on an open set need not attain its minimum ($x$ on $(0,1)$). Non-convex functions <em>can</em> have a unique global minimum ($\sqrt{|x|}$), and can have several global minima ($(x-1)^2(x+1)^2$).`,
  R`<b>The standard classification list.</b> $e^{-x}$: convex ($f''=e^{-x}>0$), no minimizer. $x$: convex and concave. $x^2$: strictly convex, unique min. $x^3$: neither ($f''=6x$ changes sign). $|x|$: convex but not strictly (linear pieces) and not differentiable at 0. $|x|^3$: convex ($f''=6|x|\ge0$), and strictly convex since the chord inequality is strict. $-|x|$, $-x^2$: concave. $\sum_i(x_i-a_i)^2$: strictly convex. $x^2-y^2$ and $xy$: neither. $\log(1+e^x)$ (softplus), $\max(0,x)$ (ReLU): convex.`,
  R`<b>Worked counterexample.</b> $f(x)=(x-1)^2(x+1)^2=(x^2-1)^2$: $f'=4x(x^2-1)$ vanishes at $0,\pm1$; $f''=12x^2-4$ is $-4$ at $0$ so $f$ is not convex, $x=0$ is a local max, and $x=\pm1$ are two distinct global minima with $f=0$. This is the go-to example for "local min is global" failing without convexity and for "global min need not be unique".`,
  R`<b>Quadratic forms.</b> $l(\theta)=(\theta-\theta^\ast)^tB(\theta-\theta^\ast)$ with $B=B^t\succ0$ is strictly convex with unique minimizer $\theta^\ast$; eigen-decomposition $B=U\Sigma U^t$, positive definite $\iff$ all eigenvalues $\sigma_i>0$. If $B$ is only non-negative definite the function is convex but the minimizer is a whole affine set. This is the model problem for the gradient-descent conditioning questions. (Week 2 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/prob"] = [
  R`<b>Probability space.</b> A triple $(\Omega,\mathcal B,P)$: $\Omega$ the sample space, $\mathcal B$ the "well behaved" subsets (events; the power set $2^\Omega$ when $\Omega$ is discrete), $P(A)$ the probability of event $A$. Axioms: $P(\Omega)=1$; $P(A)\ge0$; for pairwise disjoint $A_i$, $P(\bigcup_iA_i)=\sum_iP(A_i)$. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Random variable.</b> A (measurable) function $X:\Omega\to\mathbb R$, thought of as the outcome of an experiment. $\{X\le\lambda\}$ is shorthand for the <em>event</em> $\{\omega\in\Omega:X(\omega)\le\lambda\}$, and $P\{X=2\}=P(\{\omega:X(\omega)=2\})$. Any function of a random variable, $f(X)$, is again a random variable. $X$ itself is <em>not</em> a number.`,
  R`<b>Discrete density, expectation, variance.</b> $P\{X=i\}=p_i$, $i=0,\dots,M-1$, with $p_i\ge0$, $\sum_ip_i=1$, so $p\in\mathcal S_M$ (the simplex). $P\{X\in A\}=\sum_{i\in A}p_i$. $\mu=E[X]=\sum_nn\,p_n$; $E[f(X)]=\sum_nf(n)p_n$; $\sigma^2=E[(X-\mu)^2]=\sum_n(n-\mu)^2p_n$. Expectations are numbers.`,
  R`<b>Joint, marginal, conditional.</b> $P\{X=i,Y=j\}=p(i,j)$; marginals $p_X(i)=\sum_jp(i,j)$, $p_Y(j)=\sum_ip(i,j)$; conditionals $p_{X|Y}(i|j)=p(i,j)/p_Y(j)=p(i,j)/\sum_ip(i,j)$ and symmetrically $p_{Y|X}$. Bayes' law: $p_{X|Y}(x|y)=\dfrac{p_{Y|X}(y|x)p_X(x)}{\sum_kp_{Y|X}(y|k)p_X(k)}$.`,
  R`<b>Conditional expectation is a random variable.</b> $E[f(X)|Y]=\sum_nf(n)\,p_{X|Y}(n|Y)$ is a function of $Y$, hence random; $E[f(Y)|X]$ is a function of $X$. Slide examples: $X^2$ random; $E[X^2]$ a number; $E[Y|X]$ random; $E[X|X]=X$; $E[YX|X]=X\,E[Y|X]$ (pull out what is known).`,
  R`<b>Rules to have memorized.</b> Tower property $E[E[Y|X]]=E[Y]$; linearity; if $Y$ and $Z$ are independent then $E[Y|Z]=E[Y]$ and $E[YZ]=E[Y]E[Z]$; $E[g(Z)X|Z]=g(Z)E[X|Z]$; $E[c|Y]=c$. Independence of $X$ and $Y$ means $p(i,j)=p_X(i)p_Y(j)$ for all $i,j$.`,
  R`<b>MMSE estimator.</b> Among all functions $T(Y)$, the one minimizing $E[(X-T(Y))^2]$ is $T^\ast(Y)=E[X|Y]$. Sketch: write $X-T=(X-E[X|Y])+(E[X|Y]-T)$; the cross term vanishes because $E[(X-E[X|Y])h(Y)]=E[h(Y)E[X-E[X|Y]\,|\,Y]]=0$ for any $h$, so $E[(X-T)^2]=E[(X-E[X|Y])^2]+E[(E[X|Y]-T)^2]\ge E[(X-E[X|Y])^2]$. The MMSE estimate $\hat X=E[X|Y]$ is the Bayesian estimator behind MSE-trained networks.`,
  R`<b>$E[f(X)]$ need not exist.</b> The sum/integral must converge absolutely; heavy-tailed densities (e.g. $p(z)\propto(1+|z|)^{-3}$ with $f(x)=x^3$, or the Cauchy density with $f(x)=x$) give $\infty-\infty$. So "does $E[f(X)]$ always exist?" is answered <em>no</em>.`,
  R`<b>Frequentist estimation.</b> $\theta$ is a deterministic unknown; data $(X,Y)\sim p_\theta(x,y)$; an estimator is $\hat\theta=T(X,Y)$. Unbiased means $E[\hat\theta|\theta]=\theta$. $\mathrm{bias}=E[\hat\theta|\theta]-\theta$, $\mathrm{Var}=E[(\hat\theta-E[\hat\theta|\theta])^2|\theta]$, $\mathrm{MSE}=\mathrm{Var}+\mathrm{bias}^2$. Example: the sample mean of $N$ i.i.d. draws is unbiased with variance $\sigma^2/N$; the ML estimate is (mostly) unbiased and asymptotically efficient (achieves the Cramer-Rao bound).`,
  R`<b>Bayesian estimation.</b> $X$ is random with prior $p_X(x)$ and forward model $p_{Y|X}(y|x)$; estimator $\hat X=f(Y)$. MMSE: $\hat X=E[X|Y]$. MAP: $\hat x=\arg\max_xp_{X|Y}(x|y)=\arg\min_x\{-\log p_{Y|X}(y|x)-\log p_X(x)\}$ (the $\log p_Y(y)$ term drops). Inference is typically Bayesian and <em>always biased</em>; training is typically frequentist and unbiased unless regularized. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Bias-variance tradeoff.</b> Variance $\leftrightarrow$ noise; bias $\leftrightarrow$ excessive smoothing, regularization, blur. As bias $\to0$, variance $\to\infty$: you cannot estimate with infinite resolution from finite data. Bayesian estimates are high-bias/low-variance (best when the prior is strong or data are few/poor); frequentist estimates are low-bias/high-variance (best with weak priors and lots of good data). Regularization deliberately introduces bias to cut variance.`,
  R`<b>Independence of events.</b> $A,B$ independent iff $P(A\cap B)=P(A)P(B)$. On $\Omega=[0,1]$ with uniform $P$: $A=[0,\tfrac12]$, $B=[\tfrac14,\tfrac34]$ are independent ($P(A\cap B)=\tfrac14=\tfrac12\cdot\tfrac12$) while $A=[0,\tfrac12]$, $B=[\tfrac12,1]$ are not ($P(A\cap B)=0\ne\tfrac14$). Disjoint events with positive probability are never independent; $\Omega$ and $\emptyset$ are independent of everything.`,
  R`<b>Cross-entropy minimization is maximum likelihood.</b> With one-hot labels $x_k$ and predicted class probabilities $f_\theta(y_k)\in\mathcal S_M$, $-\log p_\theta(x_k|y_k)=-\sum_mx_{k,m}\log[f_\theta(y_k)]_m$, so $\arg\min_\theta\sum_k\rho_{CE}(x_k,f_\theta(y_k))=\arg\max_\theta p_\theta(x|y)$. Likewise MSE minimization is ML under additive Gaussian noise. (Week 6 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/cnnparams"] = [
  R`<b>Why convolution instead of dense layers.</b> A dense layer on an $N\times N$ image needs $\sim N^4$ weights ($10^{12}$ for a $1024\times1024$ image), so training and inference are slow, and many image problems should be <em>space-invariant</em> (object recognition should not depend on position; denoising should depend on content, not location). Convolution gives far fewer parameters, spatial invariance (except at boundaries), a $C$-dimensional feature vector per pixel, and lets the CNN design its own features. (Week 5 slides)`,
  R`<b>Standard convolution.</b> 1-D: $x(j)=(z*w)(j)=\sum_kz(j-k)w(k)=\sum_kw(j-k)z(k)$, commutative. 2-D: $x(j_1,j_2)=\sum_{k_1}\sum_{k_2}z(j_1-k_1,j_2-k_2)w(k_1,k_2)$ with a $(2p+1)\times(2p+1)$ kernel. DNN software actually implements <em>correlation</em> $\sum_kw(k-j)z(k)=w(-j)*z(j)$, which is not commutative; time-reverse $w$ to convert. The course uses true convolution for all analysis.`,
  R`<b>"Same" vs "valid" boundary.</b> <em>Same</em>: zero-pad the input by $p$ on each side so the output has the same $N\times N$ size. <em>Valid</em>: only compute outputs where the kernel lies entirely inside the input, so each spatial dimension shrinks by $k-1=2p$: $N_{out}=N_{in}-k+1$. Stride $s$ (decimation) further gives $N_{out}=\lfloor(N_{in}-k)/s\rfloor+1$ for valid, or $N/s$ for same.`,
  R`<b>Multichannel (vector) convolution in tensor notation.</b> $x_{j_1,j_2,j_3}=w_{j_1,j_2,i,j_3}*z_{j_1,j_2,i}$, meaning $x(j_1,j_2,j_3)=\sum_{i=0}^{C_{in}-1}\sum_{k_1,k_2}z(j_1-k_1,j_2-k_2,i)\,w(k_1,k_2,i,j_3)$: sum over the input channel index $i$ as well as the spatial taps. Each output channel $j_3$ has its own $k\times k\times C_{in}$ filter bank.`,
  R`<b>Tensor shapes and ranks.</b> Input/feature tensor $z$: $N\times N\times C_{in}$ (rank 3). Kernel $w$: $k\times k\times C_{in}\times C_{out}$ (rank 4). Offset $b$: $C_{out}$ (rank 1), one scalar per output channel broadcast over all pixels. Output $x$: $N_{out}\times N_{out}\times C_{out}$. Slide example: $w$ is $5\times5\times16\times16=6{,}400$ numbers while $z$ is $512\times512\times16=4{,}194{,}304$.`,
  R`<b>Parameter count of a conv layer.</b> $\#\text{params}=k^2C_{in}C_{out}+C_{out}$ (weights plus one offset per output channel). Slide 5-layer CNN with $5\times5$ kernels: layer 1 ($1\to16$): $400+16=416$; layers 2-4 ($16\to16$): $6400+16=6{,}416$ each; layer 5 ($16\to1$): $400+1=401$; total $20{,}065$. ReLU, pooling, stride and upsampling have <em>no</em> parameters.`,
  R`<b>Single-layer CNN.</b> $x=f_\theta(z)=\sigma(w*z+b)$, i.e. $x_{j_1,j_2,j_3}=\sigma(w_{j_1,j_2,i,j_3}*z_{j_1,j_2,i}+b_{j_3})$, with $\theta=(w,b)$. A deep CNN is a chain of these; the interior state at every pixel is a $C$-vector of learned features.`,
  R`<b>The standard two-layer counting procedure.</b> Given $N_0\times N_0\times C_0$ input, $k\times k$ kernels, valid boundaries, channels $C_0\to C_1\to C_2$: $N_1=N_0-(k-1)$, $N_2=N_1-(k-1)$; $w_1\in\mathbb R^{k\times k\times C_0\times C_1}$, $b_1\in\mathbb R^{C_1}$, $w_2\in\mathbb R^{k\times k\times C_1\times C_2}$, $b_2\in\mathbb R^{C_2}$; total $k^2C_0C_1+C_1+k^2C_1C_2+C_2$. Example (2025): $128\times128$, $k=5$, $1\to8\to3$: $N_1=124$, $N_2=120$, params $208+603=811$.`,
  R`<b>Stride, upsampling, pooling.</b> Stride $(d_1,d_2)$: $x(j_1,j_2)=z(d_1j_1,d_2j_2)$, output $N_1/d_1\times N_2/d_2$ (decimation). Upsampling by $(L_1,L_2)$: $x(j_1,j_2)=z(\lfloor j_1/L_1\rfloor,\lfloor j_2/L_2\rfloor)$ (pixel replication), output $L_1N_1\times L_2N_2$. Average pooling: $x(j)=\tfrac1{p^2}\sum_{k_1,k_2=0}^{p-1}z(d_1j_1+k_1,d_2j_2+k_2)$; max pooling replaces the mean by $\max$. Pooling with stride $p$ shrinks by $p$; none have parameters.`,
  R`<b>Einstein notation.</b> Any index appearing twice is summed: $x=g_jy^j=\sum_jg_jy^j$. <em>Contravariant</em> (upper-index, column) vectors are data; <em>covariant</em> (lower-index, row) vectors operate on data, e.g. gradients. A matrix is a rank-2 tensor $x^i=G^i_jy^j$; a 2-D image to 2-D image operator is rank 4, $x^{i_1,i_2}=G^{i_1,i_2}_{j_1,j_2}y^{j_1,j_2}$. Rank $=$ number of indices. Useful identities: $\delta^i_j$, $\nabla_y(Ay)=A$, $[\nabla_A(Ay)]^i_{j_1,j_2}=\delta^i_{j_1}y^{j_2}$. (Week 3 slides)`,
  R`<b>Plain CNN good news / bad news.</b> Good: dramatic parameter reduction, spatial invariance, per-pixel feature vectors, self-designed features. Bad: every layer keeps the same spatial resolution, ReLUs are fast but can die, and an image-sized output is wrong for classification, hence pooling/strides to shrink, then a dense (fully connected) head and softmax.`,
  R`<b>Valid regions shrink fast.</b> With valid boundaries each $5\times5$ layer removes 4 pixels: $80\to76\to72\to\dots\to20$ after 15 layers. For deep CNNs the valid region tends to zero, which is why "same" padding is standard inside deep nets and why training on small patches does not speed training or add data (an apocryphal belief the slides debunk). (Week 9 slides)`,
  R`<b>Common mistakes.</b> Forgetting the $+C_{out}$ offsets; using $N-k$ instead of $N-k+1$; counting parameters per pixel (weights are shared across positions); giving pooling or ReLU parameters; mixing up which channel count multiplies which; writing the kernel tensor as $C_{out}\times C_{in}\times k\times k$ and losing track of rank (still rank 4, still $k^2C_{in}C_{out}$ numbers).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/adjoint"] = [
  R`<b>Convolution is a matrix.</b> $x(i)=(w*z)(i)=\sum_jw(i-j)z(j)$ is $x=Az$ with $A_{i,j}=w(i-j)$, so $\partial x_i/\partial z_j=A_{i,j}=w(i-j)$: a Toeplitz matrix whose rows are shifted copies of the reversed kernel. (Week 5 slides)`,
  R`<b>Adjoint w.r.t. the input.</b> $A^t_{i,j}=A_{j,i}=w(j-i)$, so $g_\delta(i)=\sum_jw(j-i)\epsilon(j)=(w(-n)*\epsilon)(i)$: the adjoint of convolution with $w$ is convolution with the <em>time-reversed</em> impulse response $w(-n)$ (equivalently correlation with $w$). This is the back-propagated error $\delta=A^t\epsilon$.`,
  R`<b>Adjoint w.r.t. the weights.</b> Viewing the same output as linear in $w$: $x(i)=\sum_jz(i-j)w(j)$, so $x=C w$ with $C_{i,j}=z(i-j)$; the adjoint gives $g_w(j)=\sum_iz(i-j)\epsilon(i)$: correlate the error $\epsilon$ with the input $z$ (the slide says "autocorrelates $\epsilon$ with the time reverse of $z$"). Its output has the size of the kernel.`,
  R`<b>The 1-D valid toy problem.</b> Kernel $(w_0,w_1,w_2)$, input $y\in\mathbb R^N$, valid output $x_n=\sum_{k=0}^2w_ky_{n+2-k}=w_0y_{n+2}+w_1y_{n+1}+w_2y_n$ for $n=0,\dots,N-3$. Then $x=A_wy$ with $A_w\in\mathbb R^{(N-2)\times N}$, row $n$ equal to $[0\cdots0,\,w_2,w_1,w_0,\,0\cdots0]$ with $w_2$ in column $n$. Check: number of rows $=$ number of outputs, columns $=$ inputs.`,
  R`<b>Its transpose.</b> $A_w^t\in\mathbb R^{N\times(N-2)}$ has column $n$ equal to row $n$ of $A_w$; $\delta=A_w^t\epsilon$ gives $\delta_m=w_2\epsilon_m+w_1\epsilon_{m-1}+w_0\epsilon_{m-2}$ (terms with out-of-range index dropped): zero-pad $\epsilon$ by 2 on each side and convolve with the reversed kernel $(w_2,w_1,w_0)$, a "full" convolution that grows $N-2$ back to $N$. Interpretation: each output error is distributed back to the three inputs that produced it, weighted by the taps used.`,
  R`<b>The weight matrix $C_y$ and its transpose.</b> Writing $x=C_yw$: $C_y\in\mathbb R^{(N-2)\times3}$ with row $n=[y_{n+2},y_{n+1},y_n]$. Then $g_w=C_y^t\epsilon$, $[g_w]_k=\sum_n\epsilon_ny_{n+2-k}$, $k=0,1,2$: the valid correlation of the error with the input, exactly 3 numbers, the gradient of the loss w.r.t. the kernel taps.`,
  R`<b>Gradient of the MSE loss through the block.</b> For $L(y)=\tfrac1K\sum_k\|x_k-f(y)\|^2$ with $f(y)=A_wy$ (or $A_wy+b$): $\nabla_yL=-\tfrac2K\sum_k(x_k-f(y))^tA_w$ (a $1\times N$ row) and $[\nabla_yL]^t=A_w^t\epsilon$ where $\epsilon=-\tfrac2K\sum_k(x_k-f(y))$: average the errors, then apply the adjoint. Same pattern with $C_y^t$ for the gradient w.r.t. $w$.`,
  R`<b>2-D multichannel versions from the slides.</b> For $f(z)=\sigma(w_{j_1,j_2,i,j_3}*z_{j_1,j_2,i}+b_{j_3})$: input gradient $\delta_{j_1,j_2,i}=w_{-j_1,-j_2,i,j_3}*[\nabla\sigma\,\epsilon]_{j_1,j_2,j_3}$ (reverse the kernel spatially, swap in/out channel roles, sum over $j_3$); offset gradient $g_b(j_3)=\sum_{j_1,j_2}[\nabla\sigma\,\epsilon]_{j_1,j_2,j_3}$ (sum over all pixels); weight gradient $g_w(j_1,j_2,i,j_3)=z_{-j_1,-j_2,i}*[\nabla\sigma\,\epsilon]_{j_1,j_2,j_3}$ (correlation of error with input, per channel pair).`,
  R`<b>"Same" boundary with circular wrap.</b> $A$ becomes circulant, $A_{i,j}=w((i-j)\bmod N)$; its transpose is circulant with the reversed kernel, $A^t_{i,j}=w((j-i)\bmod N)$, i.e. circular convolution with $w(-n)$. With zero padding instead, $A^t$ is still "convolution with the reversed kernel" but the boundary rows are truncated.`,
  R`<b>Forward and backward functions.</b> Each node needs $x\leftarrow F(z,\theta)$ (forward) and $\delta,g\leftarrow G(\epsilon,z,\theta)$ that returns $A^t\epsilon$ and $B^t\epsilon$. <em>You never form $A$ or $A^t$</em>: the fast adjoint gradient computes the products directly with convolutions/correlations. (Week 4-5 slides)`,
  R`<b>Linear vs nonlinear layers.</b> For the linear block $x=Az$ the backward map $\delta=A^t\epsilon$ does not depend on $z$. With an activation, $\nabla_z\sigma(Az+b)=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma'(Az+b))A$, so the backward pass needs the stored forward activations (the $\nabla\sigma$ factor).`,
  R`<b>Sanity checks.</b> Shapes: $A_w$ is (outputs)$\times$(inputs), $A_w^t$ the reverse; $\delta$ has the size of the input, $g_w$ the size of the kernel. Inner-product test: $\langle A_wy,\epsilon\rangle=\langle y,A_w^t\epsilon\rangle$. Convolution commutes ($z*w=w*z$) but correlation does not, so be explicit about which signal is reversed.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/gd"] = [
  R`<b>Gradient descent.</b> Downhill direction $d=-\nabla L(\theta)$ (the gradient is a $1\times P$ row vector). Algorithm: repeat $\{d\leftarrow-\nabla L(\theta);\ \theta\leftarrow\theta+\alpha d^t\}$ until converged. Simplest and surprisingly effective, but sensitive to the step size $\alpha$. (Week 2 slides)`,
  R`<b>Step size.</b> $\alpha$ too small $\Rightarrow$ slow convergence; too large $\Rightarrow$ oscillation/instability; often there is no good single choice. Steepest descent picks $\alpha^\ast=\arg\min_\alpha L(\theta+\alpha d^t)$ by line search (too expensive for DL); coordinate descent updates one parameter at a time (many cheap updates, no global step-size problem).`,
  R`<b>Linear algebra background.</b> $B$ symmetric $\iff B=B^t$; positive definite $\iff x^tBx>0$ for all $x\ne0$; non-negative definite $\iff x^tBx\ge0$. SVD $B=U\Sigma V^t$; for symmetric $B$ the eigendecomposition $B=U\Sigma U^t$ with $\Sigma=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_0,\dots,\sigma_{p-1})$ and $U$ unitary. $B\succ0\iff\sigma_i>0$ for all $i$.`,
  R`<b>Quadratic model problem.</b> $l(\theta)=(\theta-\theta^\ast)^tB(\theta-\theta^\ast)$, $B\succ0$: strictly positive except at its unique minimizer $\theta^\ast$; WLOG $\theta^\ast=0$. Contours are ellipses with principal axes along the eigenvectors of $B$ and semi-axis lengths $\propto1/\sqrt{\sigma_i}$: long axis along the <em>smallest</em> eigenvalue, short axis along the largest.`,
  R`<b>Condition number.</b> $\kappa=\sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min}$. $\kappa=4$ looks mildly elliptical; $\kappa=100$ is a narrow valley. The steep direction (large $\sigma$) requires a small step; the flat direction (small $\sigma$) requires a large step, so no $\alpha$ suits both: GD zig-zags across the valley and creeps along it. In 1-D, the step size should scale like $1/f''$. (Week 2 slides)`,
  R`<b>Stability condition derivation.</b> For $f(\theta)=\tfrac12\theta^tH\theta$, $\nabla f=H\theta$ and GD gives $\theta_{k+1}=(I-\alpha H)\theta_k$. In the eigenbasis each component evolves as $(1-\alpha\lambda_i)^k$, so convergence requires $|1-\alpha\lambda_i|&lt;1$ for every $i$, i.e. $$0&lt;\alpha&lt;\frac{2}{\lambda_{\max}}.$$ At $\alpha=2/\lambda_{\max}$ the stiff mode oscillates with constant amplitude; beyond it diverges.`,
  R`<b>Rate of convergence.</b> With the largest safe step $\alpha\approx1/\lambda_{\max}$ the slow mode contracts by $1-\lambda_{\min}/\lambda_{\max}=1-1/\kappa$ per iteration, so the number of iterations scales with $\kappa$. Sketch questions: draw the ellipses, mark the negative gradient (perpendicular to the contour, pointing mostly across the valley), and note that the small-eigenvalue direction $(1-\alpha\lambda_{\min})^k$ converges slowly.`,
  R`<b>Worked example.</b> $f(x)=a(x_1+x_2)^2+b(x_1-x_2)^2$ has $H=\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b&2a-2b\\2a-2b&2a+2b\end{bmatrix}$ with eigenvectors $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ and eigenvalues $4a$ and $4b$: $\kappa=a/b$ (for $a>b$), stable iff $\alpha&lt;2/(4a)=1/(2a)$, and the $(1,-1)$ direction converges slowly at rate $1-4b\alpha$.`,
  R`<b>Newton's method.</b> Correct for the local second derivative: $\theta\leftarrow\theta-H^{-1}\nabla f^t$ "spheres" the ellipse and solves a quadratic in one step, but requires forming/inverting the Hessian: too much computation and too hard to implement for DL. Alternatives: preconditioning (easy, somewhat ad hoc) and momentum (Week 9).`,
  R`<b>Preconditioned gradient descent.</b> Pick any positive-definite $M$; repeat $\{g\leftarrow-\nabla L(\theta)^t;\ d\leftarrow Mg;\ \theta\leftarrow\theta+\alpha d\}$. Since $\langle d,g\rangle=g^tMg>0$ the angle between $d$ and $g$ is less than $90^\circ$, so PGD always goes downhill. Choices: $M=B^tB$, or diagonal $M=\mathrm{diag}(a_0,\dots,a_{N-1})$ with $a_i>0$. (Week 9 slides)`,
  R`<b>Preconditioning the quadratic.</b> $\theta\leftarrow\theta-\alpha MH\theta$: the effective matrix is $MH$. $M=H^{-1}$, $\alpha=1$ gives $\theta\leftarrow0$ in one step (Newton). If $H$ is diagonal, $M=\mathrm{diag}(1/H_{ii})$ is exact and cheap; in general a diagonal $M$ rescales coordinates so the ellipse becomes rounder and $\kappa$ of $MH$ drops. Adam builds such a diagonal preconditioner from the running RMS of each gradient component.`,
  R`<b>Exact line search on a quadratic.</b> For $d=-\nabla f^t$ and $f=\tfrac12\theta^tH\theta$, $\alpha^\ast=\dfrac{d^td}{d^tHd}$ (minimize the 1-D quadratic in $\alpha$). Successive steepest-descent directions are orthogonal, which is the source of the zig-zag picture.`,
  R`<b>Why this matters for deep nets.</b> The adjoint gradient of an $M$-layer chain is a product $A_0^tA_1^t\cdots A_{M-1}^t$; if all $A_m$ share a symmetric structure $U\Sigma U^t$ the product is $U\Sigma^MU^t$ with condition number $(\sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min})^M\to\infty$: the <em>vanishing gradient</em> problem is ill-conditioning. Partial fixes: Adam, skip connections, batch normalization. (Week 11 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/mle"] = [
  R`<b>Standard vs one-hot encoding.</b> Standard: the class is an integer $\hat x\in\{0,\dots,M-1\}$. One-hot: $\hat x\in\mathbb R^M$ with $\hat x_i=1$ if class $=i$ and $0$ otherwise (e.g. $M=5$, class 2 $\Rightarrow(0,0,1,0,0)$). Advantages: no spurious ordering or distance between classes (all pairs are $\sqrt2$ apart), the target lives in the same space as a probability vector, and it is what softmax/cross-entropy need. Cost: $M$ numbers instead of one. (Week 1 slides)`,
  R`<b>The simplex.</b> $\mathcal S_M=\{x\in\mathbb R^M:\ x_i\ge0\ \forall i,\ \sum_{i=0}^{M-1}x_i=1\}$. Proof of convexity: for $p,q\in\mathcal S_M$ and $\lambda\in[0,1]$, $\lambda p_i+(1-\lambda)q_i\ge0$ and $\sum_i(\lambda p_i+(1-\lambda)q_i)=\lambda+(1-\lambda)=1$. It is also closed and bounded (compact). Continuous encoding $\hat x\in\mathcal S_M$ is "like a probability density for each class": a continuous function on a convex set, which makes optimization easier and represents uncertainty.`,
  R`<b>Softmax.</b> $\sigma_i(z)=\dfrac{e^{z_i}}{\sum_je^{z_j}}$, a <em>joint</em> (not pointwise) activation. Each entry is strictly positive and they sum to one, so $\sigma(z)$ lies in the <em>interior</em> of $\mathcal S_M$ (it can approach but never reach a vertex). Invariant to adding a constant to all $z_i$. Its gradient matrix is dense: $[\nabla\sigma(z)]_{i,j}=\sigma_i(z)\delta_{i-j}-\sigma_i(z)\sigma_j(z)$, versus the sparse diagonal gradient of a pointwise activation. (Week 1 slides)`,
  R`<b>Maximum likelihood estimate.</b> Model $(X,Y)\sim p_\theta(x,y)$ with deterministic unknown $\theta$: $\hat\theta=\arg\max_\theta p_\theta(X,Y)=\arg\min_\theta\{-\log p_\theta(X,Y)\}$, usually found from $\nabla_\theta\log p_\theta(X,Y)=0$. Since $\log p_\theta(x,y)=\log p_\theta(x|y)+\log p(y)$ and the second term does not depend on $\theta$, ML training minimizes $-\sum_k\log p_\theta(x_k|y_k)$ over i.i.d. pairs. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Classification likelihood with one-hot labels.</b> The network outputs $f_\theta(m;y)=P\{\text{class}=m\,|\,Y=y\}$. With $x_{k,m}=\delta(m-m_k^\ast)$, $$p_\theta(x_k|y_k)=\prod_{m=0}^{M-1}[f_\theta(y_k)]_m^{x_{k,m}},\qquad -\log p_\theta(x_k|y_k)=-\sum_mx_{k,m}\log[f_\theta(y_k)]_m=\rho_{CE}(x_k,f_\theta(y_k)),$$ where only one term of the sum is nonzero. Summing over independent pairs, $-\log p_\theta(x|y)=K\cdot L_{CE}(\theta)$: <em>cross-entropy loss $\Leftrightarrow$ maximum likelihood</em>.`,
  R`<b>Cross-entropy as a distortion.</b> $\rho(a,b)=-\sum_ia_i\log b_i$ for $a\ge0$, $b_i>0$, $\sum_ib_i=1$ (so $b\in\mathcal S$). Its error vector for back-propagation is $\epsilon=-a./b$ (pointwise division). Software (PyTorch CrossEntropyLoss) folds the softmax in and takes logits, which avoids $\log0$. (Week 4 slides)`,
  R`<b>Multinomial ML: the empirical frequency.</b> $Y_0,\dots,Y_{N-1}$ i.i.d. with $P_\theta(Y_n=i)=\theta_i$, $\theta\in\mathcal S_M$: $p_\theta(y)=\prod_i\theta_i^{N_i}$ with counts $N_i=\sum_n\delta(y_n=i)$. Then $-\tfrac1N\log p_\theta(y)=-\sum_i\tfrac{N_i}N\log\theta_i=\mathrm{CrossEntropy}(N/N,\theta)$, convex in $\theta$, and the constrained minimizer (Lagrange multiplier for $\sum\theta_i=1$) is $\hat\theta_i=N_i/N$. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Same problem through the softmax.</b> Parametrize $\theta=\sigma(\phi)$ with free logits $\phi$: $L(\phi)=\sum_mN_m(-\phi_m+\log\sum_je^{\phi_j})$, $\partial L/\partial\phi_m=-N_m+N\sigma_m(\phi)$. Setting it to zero gives $\sigma(\phi)=N_m/N$, i.e. $\phi_m^\ast=\log N_m+\beta$ for any constant $\beta$ (the minimizer is a line, from shift invariance). Per-sample gradient of CE w.r.t. logits: $\sigma(z)-x$.`,
  R`<b>The simplex-gradient paradox.</b> Let $f_\theta(y)=\theta\in\mathcal S_3$ directly and $x=(a,a,a)$; then $L=-a\sum_i\log\theta_i$ and $\nabla_\theta L=-a/\theta_i$, which at the "obvious" answer $\theta=(\tfrac13,\tfrac13,\tfrac13)$ equals $(-3a,-3a,-3a)\ne0$. Resolution: the gradient is perpendicular to the constraint set $\sum\theta_i=1$; you must project onto the simplex or, better, parametrize with a softmax so that unconstrained GD stays feasible. (Week 4 slides)`,
  R`<b>Error vectors for the standard losses.</b> General $L=\tfrac1K\sum_k\rho(x_k,f_\theta(y_k))$ has $\nabla L^t=\tfrac1K\sum_k\nabla f_\theta(y_k)^t\epsilon_k$ with $\epsilon_k=[\nabla_b\rho(a,b)]^t$ at $a=x_k$, $b=f_\theta(y_k)$. MSE $\rho=\|a-b\|^2$: $\epsilon=-2(x-f)$. MAE $\rho=\|a-b\|_1$: $\epsilon=-\mathrm{sign}(x-f)$ (robust to outliers). CE: $\epsilon=-x./f$.`,
  R`<b>Why not MSE for classification.</b> MSE excessively weights outliers and treats one-hot targets as arbitrary vectors; CE is the negative log-likelihood of the categorical model, it is convex in the logits for a linear last layer, and its gradient $\sigma(z)-x$ never saturates the way squared error through a softmax does.`,
  R`<b>The big idea about ML.</b> Pure and ideal: uses only the data, needs no prior; (mostly) unbiased; asymptotically efficient (achieves the Cramer-Rao bound); the method of choice when data are plentiful. But it tends to overfit when data are scarce relative to the number of parameters, which motivates regularization / MAP. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Companion fact.</b> Under $X_k=f_\theta(Y_k)+W_k$, $W_k\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, $-\log p_\theta(x|y)=\tfrac1{2\sigma^2}\sum_k\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2+\text{const}$, so <em>MSE loss $\Leftrightarrow$ ML</em> for regression exactly as CE $\Leftrightarrow$ ML for classification.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/gradcomplex"] = [
  R`<b>The loss.</b> $L_{MSE}(\theta)=\tfrac1K\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2=\tfrac1K\sum_k\sum_i(x_{k,i}-[f_\theta(y_k)]_i)^2$ over $K$ training pairs. Technically "total squared error" (MSE is the expectation it approximates). $L=0$ iff $x_k=f_\theta(y_k)$ for every $k$. (Week 1 slides)`,
  R`<b>Chain-rule gradient.</b> $$\nabla_\theta L=-\frac2K\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}(x_k-f_\theta(y_k))^t\,\nabla_\theta f_\theta(y_k).$$ Three ingredients: the sum over training data (big but straightforward), the prediction error (easy), and the gradient of the inference function (the hard part, done by automatic differentiation / back-propagation). (Week 2 slides)`,
  R`<b>Shapes.</b> $A=\nabla_\theta f_\theta(y)\in\mathbb R^{N_x\times P}$ with $A_{i,j}=\partial[f_\theta(y)]_i/\partial\theta_j$: row index $i$ is the output component, column index $j$ the parameter. The error $\epsilon_k^t=(x_k-f_\theta(y_k))^t$ is $1\times N_x$, the gradient $\nabla_\theta L$ is a $1\times P$ row vector, and the update is $\theta\leftarrow\theta+\alpha d^t$ with $d=-\nabla_\theta L$.`,
  R`<b>Forward vs backward (adjoint) evaluation.</b> Forward: $\delta\hat x\leftarrow A\,\delta\theta$ maps a parameter perturbation ($P\times1$) to an output perturbation ($N_x\times1$). Backward: $\epsilon^tA\to d$ maps an output error ($1\times N_x$) to a parameter direction ($1\times P$); transposed, $d^t=A^t\epsilon$ with $A^t\in\mathbb R^{P\times N_x}$ the <em>adjoint gradient</em>, which exchanges input and output and reverses the flow of the network.`,
  R`<b>Multiply counts for a general Jacobian.</b> One product $\epsilon_k^tA_k$ costs $N_xP$ multiplies; over $K$ samples $KN_xP$, plus $P$ more to scale the sum by $2/K$: about $KN_xP+P$. If $A$ has structure the count collapses: e.g. rank-one $A=\mathbf 1\theta^t$ gives $\epsilon^tA=(\epsilon^t\mathbf 1)\theta^t$, only $N_x+P$ multiplies per sample; convolution costs $\sim k^2$ per output instead of the full matrix.`,
  R`<b>Order of operations matters.</b> Matrix multiplication is associative but the cost is not. For a chain $A\,B\,C\,D\,E$ of alternating $N\times1$ and $1\times N$ vectors, left-to-right forms dense $N\times N$ outer products ($\sim4N^2$ multiplies) while right-to-left forms scalar inner products ($\sim4N$). (Week 3 slides)`,
  R`<b>The exam's $p\times p$ examples.</b> $a^tBC$ with $a\in\mathbb R^p$, $B,C\in\mathbb R^{p\times p}$: $(a^tB)C$ costs $p^2+p^2=2p^2$; $a^t(BC)$ costs $p^3+p^2$. $DEf$ with $f\in\mathbb R^p$: $D(Ef)$ costs $2p^2$; $(DE)f$ costs $p^3+p^2$. Rule: always multiply starting from the end that has the vector (the small dimension) so you never form a matrix-matrix product.`,
  R`<b>Why back-propagation.</b> The loss has output dimension 1, so the "vector end" of the chain $\nabla L=\epsilon^tA_{M-1}\cdots A_0$ is the error at the output. Propagating the $1\times N$ row vector backward costs $O(N^2K)$ (matrix-vector at each layer); building the Jacobians forward costs $O(N^3K)$ (matrix-matrix). Forward-mode differentiation would instead need one pass per parameter. (Week 4 slides)`,
  R`<b>Deep chain rule.</b> For $f_\theta=f_{2,\theta_2}\circ f_{1,\theta_1}\circ f_{0,\theta_0}$ with $A_m=\nabla_zf_{m}$ ($N_{m+1}\times N_m$) and $B_m=\nabla_\theta f_m$ ($N_{m+1}\times P_m$): $\nabla_{z_0}f=A_2A_1A_0$, $\nabla_{\theta_0}f=A_2A_1B_0$, $\nabla_{\theta_1}f=A_2B_1$, $\nabla_{\theta_2}f=B_2$; adjoints reverse the order, $\nabla_{\theta_0}f^t=B_0^tA_1^tA_2^t$.`,
  R`<b>Single-layer network gradients in Einstein notation.</b> $f_\theta(y)=B\sigma(Ay+b)$, $\theta=(A,b,B)$. $[\nabla_yf]^i_j=B^i_{i_1}[\nabla\sigma]^{i_1}_{i_2}A^{i_2}_j$ (a matrix); $[\nabla_Af]^i_{j_1,j_2}=B^i_{i_2}[\nabla\sigma]^{i_2}_{j_1}y^{j_2}$ (a rank-3 tensor, using $[\nabla_A(Ay)]^i_{j_1,j_2}=\delta^i_{j_1}y^{j_2}$); $[\nabla_bf]^i_{j}=B^i_{i_2}[\nabla\sigma]^{i_2}_{j}$; $[\nabla_Bf]^i_{j_1,j_2}=\delta^i_{j_1}\sigma^{j_2}$. (Week 3 slides)`,
  R`<b>Efficient update directions.</b> $d_A=\tfrac2K\sum_k\epsilon_k^tB\,\nabla\sigma_k\,(\cdot)\,y_k^t$: compute the $1\times N_1$ row $\epsilon_k^tB\nabla\sigma_k$ first (cheap matrix-vector products), then the outer product with $y_k$; $d_b=\tfrac2K\sum_k\epsilon_k^tB\nabla\sigma_k$; $d_B=\tfrac2K\sum_k\epsilon_k\sigma_k^t$. "For efficiency, computation goes this way" is the slide's arrow from the error backward.`,
  R`<b>Activation Jacobians.</b> A pointwise activation has a diagonal, sparse $N\times N$ gradient $\mathrm{diag}(\sigma'(z_i))$, fast to compute and apply (so $\nabla\sigma\,v$ is just an elementwise product). Softmax has a dense gradient $\sigma_i\delta_{i-j}-\sigma_i\sigma_j$, slow in general though tricks exist. (Week 1 slides)`,
  R`<b>General loss functions plug in through $\epsilon$.</b> For $L=\tfrac1K\sum_k\rho(x_k,f_\theta(y_k))$, $\nabla L^t=\tfrac1K\sum_k\nabla f_\theta(y_k)^t\epsilon_k$ with $\epsilon_k=[\nabla_b\rho(a,b)]^t|_{a=x_k,b=f_\theta(y_k)}$: MSE $-2(x_k-f)$, MAE $-\mathrm{sign}(x_k-f)$, CE $-x_k./f$. Only the error vector changes; the adjoint machinery is identical. (Week 4 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt1/train"] = [
  R`<b>Generalization.</b> Goal: learn the true relationship $x=f_\theta(y)+\text{error}$ from training pairs $\{(x_k,y_k)\}_{k=0}^{K-1}$ in a way that generalizes beyond them. Key quantities: model order $P=\dim\theta$ (number of parameters) and the amount of data $N_x\times K$. $P\gg N_xK$ tends to overfit; $P\ll N_xK$ tends to underfit; "Goldilocks" fitting is just right. (Week 7 slides)`,
  R`<b>Three disjoint partitions.</b> Randomly split the labeled data $S=S_T\cup S_V\cup S_E$ (training, validation, testing) with empty pairwise intersections. Each has its own loss $L_T,L_V,L_E$ computed with the same formula $\tfrac1{|S|}\sum_{k\in S}\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2$ on different data. Training data is the <em>only</em> data SGD uses: $\theta^\ast=\arg\min_\theta L_T(\theta)$.`,
  R`<b>Roles.</b> Validation compares models of different order/capacity, picks hyperparameters and the stopping epoch. Testing is sequestered for the final, unbiased evaluation. Never report $L_T$ as accuracy (the network has memorized the answers); never report $L_V$ either (it is biased by the tuning you did on it); only $L_E$.`,
  R`<b>Reading loss-vs-epoch curves.</b> $L_T$ decreases monotonically. $L_V$ decreases, reaches a minimum, then rises as the model overfits. The <em>generalization loss</em> is the gap $L_V-L_T$. Best stopping point: the epoch where $L_V$ is minimal (early termination); the model order was too high but early stopping partly fixes it.`,
  R`<b>Diagnosis 1: overfitting (capacity too high).</b> $L_V\gg L_T$, validation loss turns upward. Fixes: more training data or data augmentation, regularization ($L_1$/$L_2$ penalty $\beta S(\theta)$ with larger $\beta$ meaning less overfitting), dropout, early stopping, a smaller model.`,
  R`<b>Diagnosis 2: underfitting (capacity too low).</b> $L_V\approx L_T$ and both are high or still decreasing together. Fixes: increase model order (more layers/channels), train longer, use the final epoch. "Everything should be made as simple as possible, but no simpler."`,
  R`<b>Effect of more data.</b> Loss vs number of training pairs $K$: $L_T$ <em>increases</em> (more constraints to fit) while $L_V$ <em>decreases</em>, and the two converge. Ten times more data typically raises training loss, lowers validation loss and closes the gap. More data is always better, but slower to train.`,
  R`<b>Loss vs model order.</b> $L_T$ decreases monotonically with $P$; $L_V$ is U-shaped with a best model order/capacity at its minimum. The loss-vs-regularization curve has the same U shape in $1/\beta$.`,
  R`<b>Regularization as MAP.</b> $\tilde L(\theta)=L(\theta)+\beta S(\theta)$ is the MAP estimate with prior $p(\theta)\propto\exp\{-\tfrac\beta\sigma S(\theta)\}$: $S=\|\theta\|_2^2$ is like a Gaussian prior (shrinks weights), $S=\|\theta\|_1$ like a Laplacian prior (drives weights to exactly zero, an effective model-order reduction). It introduces bias but reduces overfitting; use it when training error $\ll$ validation error. (Week 6-7 slides)`,
  R`<b>Dropout.</b> During training, for each batch draw Bernoulli masks $r^{(l)}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)$ (retain probability $p\approx0.8$) independently per node and multiply activations by them. For validation, testing and inference, instead scale every activation by $p$. Acts like training an ensemble of thinned networks; leaving dropout on at inference turns the network into a stochastic generator. (Week 7 slides)`,
  R`<b>Data augmentation.</b> When there is not enough data, transform what you have: translation, rotation, stretching, shearing, flips, noise. It enlarges the effective training set and enforces invariances, reducing overfitting. (Week 11 slides)`
];

/* =====================================================================
   MIDTERM 2
   ===================================================================== */

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/train"] = [
  R`<b>Setup and vocabulary.</b> Training pairs $\{(x_k,y_k)\}_{k=0}^{K-1}$, model $x=f_\theta(y)+\text{error}$, model order $P=\dim\theta$, data size $N_x\times K$. Overfitting: model order too high, fits the noise, doesn't generalize. Underfitting: model order too low, cannot follow the trend. Goldilocks: just right, best generalization. (Week 7 slides)`,
  R`<b>Partition.</b> $S=S_T\cup S_V\cup S_E$, pairwise disjoint; $L_T$, $L_V$, $L_E$ are the same loss formula evaluated on each subset. Training set: the only data used by SGD, $\theta^\ast=\arg\min_\theta L_T$. Validation: compare model orders, choose hyperparameters and the epoch. Test: final evaluation, touched once.`,
  R`<b>Why a separate test set.</b> $L_T$ is like grading a homework after seeing the solutions (the network memorized them). $L_V$ is also biased because you selected the architecture, $\beta$, and stopping epoch to make it small. Only the sequestered $L_E$ is an unbiased estimate of real performance.`,
  R`<b>Curve reading, case A (overfit).</b> $L_T$ falls steadily; $L_V$ falls then rises; large gap. Model capacity too high for the data. Keep the weights from the epoch of minimum $L_V$ (early termination). Other fixes: more data / augmentation, $L_1$ or $L_2$ regularization, dropout, reduce the model.`,
  R`<b>Curve reading, case B (underfit).</b> $L_T\approx L_V$, both high or still decreasing at the end. Capacity too low (or not trained long enough). Fixes: bigger model (more layers, channels, more expressive blocks), train more epochs, keep the last epoch. Regularization would make it worse.`,
  R`<b>Effect of the amount of data.</b> Plot loss vs $K$: $L_T$ rises, $L_V$ falls, they converge as $K\to\infty$. Ten times more data: training loss up, validation loss down, gap shrinks. More data is always better but slower. If $L_T$ is tiny while $L_V$ is huge, "this is great, but this is terrible."`,
  R`<b>Loss vs model order.</b> $L_T$ decreases monotonically with $P$; $L_V$ has a minimum at the best model order/capacity. Analogously loss vs $1/\beta$ (regularization strength) shows a best $\beta$; more regularization pushes the curves together, less lets them separate.`,
  R`<b>Solutions to parameter overfitting (the slide's list).</b> (1) Early termination at the minimum of $L_V$. (2) Weight regularization $\tilde L(\theta)=L(\theta)+\beta S(\theta)$ with $S=\|\theta\|_1$ or $\|\theta\|_2^2$; larger $\beta$ means less overfitting. (3) Dropout.`,
  R`<b>Regularization is MAP estimation.</b> $\hat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\{-\log p_\theta(x,y)+\beta S(\theta)\}$ equals the MAP estimate with prior $p(\theta)=\tfrac1z\exp\{-\tfrac\beta\sigma S(\theta)\}$ ($S=-\log p(\theta)$ in general). $L_2$: Gaussian prior, reduces weight amplitudes. $L_1$: Laplacian prior, encourages weights to become exactly zero, i.e. effective model-order reduction. Introduces bias, reduces variance/overfitting; use when training error $\ll$ validation error. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Dropout training pseudo-code.</b> For each batch: for each layer $l$ draw $r^{(l)}\leftarrow\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p,\mathrm{shape}(y^{(l)}))$ once per batch; for each sample, $\tilde y^{(l)}\leftarrow r^{(l)}.*y^{(l)}$, $z^{(l+1)}\leftarrow w^{(l+1)}*\tilde y^{(l)}$, $y^{(l+1)}\leftarrow f(z^{(l+1)})$. Drop probability $1-p\approx0.2$. For validation/testing/inference replace the mask by the constant $p$ ($\tilde y\leftarrow p\,y$) to account for the larger number of active nodes. (Week 7 slides)`,
  R`<b>Why dropout regularizes.</b> Each batch trains a random thinned sub-network sharing weights, so no node can rely on a specific co-adapted partner; at test time the scaled full network approximates the ensemble average. Leaving dropout on during inference gives a stochastic generator: a deterministic input yields a random output.`,
  R`<b>Bias-variance view.</b> Overfitting is high variance (noise); regularization trades it for bias (blur/smoothing). As bias $\to0$, variance $\to\infty$ with finite data. Bayesian/regularized estimation is preferred when data are few or noisy; pure ML when data are plentiful. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Other practical regularizers.</b> Data augmentation (translate, rotate, stretch, shear) when there is not enough data; batch normalization (adds multiplicative noise per batch); weight initialization; transfer learning from a pretrained encoder. Batch norm is best when outputs are small or need no high relative accuracy; turn off its adaptation for final training. (Week 11 slides)`,
  R`<b>Checklist for the exam plot.</b> Identify which curve is $L_T$ (the lower, monotone one). Locate the minimum of $L_V$ and name that epoch. State the gap and diagnose capacity. Prescribe a fix consistent with the diagnosis. Say what $10\times$ data would do to each curve. State which set SGD used (training) and which is reported (test).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/backprop"] = [
  R`<b>Deep structure.</b> $f_\theta(y)=f_{M-1,\theta_{M-1}}\circ\cdots\circ f_{0,\theta_0}(y)$ with hidden states $z_0=y$, $z_{m+1}=f_{m,\theta_m}(z_m)$, $\hat x=z_M$, and $\theta=(\theta_0,\dots,\theta_{M-1})$. Deep (10-100 layers) beats shallow empirically, but all layers must be trained jointly, which requires automatic differentiation and raises the vanishing-gradient problem. (Week 4 slides)`,
  R`<b>Per-layer Jacobians.</b> Input gradient $A_m=\nabla_zf_{\theta_m}(z_m)\in\mathbb R^{N_{m+1}\times N_m}$ with $[A_m]_{i,j}=\partial[f_{\theta_m}]_i/\partial[z_m]_j$; parameter gradient $B_m=\nabla_\theta f_{\theta_m}(z_m)\in\mathbb R^{N_{m+1}\times P_m}$. Adjoints: $A_m^t\in\mathbb R^{N_m\times N_{m+1}}$, $B_m^t\in\mathbb R^{P_m\times N_{m+1}}$.`,
  R`<b>Forward gradient propagation (chain rule).</b> $\nabla_{z_0}f_\theta=A_{M-1}\cdots A_1A_0$; $\nabla_{\theta_m}f_\theta=A_{M-1}\cdots A_{m+1}B_m$. Perturbations flow forward: $\delta z_{m+1}=A_m\delta z_m+B_m\delta\theta_m$, so $\delta\hat x=A_1A_0\delta z_0$ and $\delta\hat x=A_1B_0\delta\theta_0$ in a two-layer net.`,
  R`<b>Back-propagation of the adjoint gradient.</b> For each training pair, $g_{m,k}=\nabla_{\theta_m}\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2{}^t=B_m^tA_{m+1}^t\cdots A_{M-1}^t\,\epsilon_k$ with error vector $\epsilon_k=-2(x_k-f_\theta(y_k))$. Define $\epsilon_{m}=A_m^t\epsilon_{m+1}$ (with $\epsilon_M=\epsilon_k$) and $g_{m,k}=B_m^t\epsilon_{m+1}$. The loss gradient is $\nabla_{\theta_m}L=\tfrac1K\sum_kg_{m,k}$.`,
  R`<b>Pseudo-code.</b> Forward: $z_0\leftarrow y$; for $m=0..M-1$: $z_{m+1}\leftarrow f_{\theta_m}(z_m)$; $\hat x\leftarrow z_M$. Backward: $g_m\leftarrow0$; for each $k$: $\epsilon\leftarrow-2(x_k-f_\theta(y_k))$; for $m=M-1$ down to $0$: $A\leftarrow\nabla_zf_{\theta_m}(z_m)$, $B\leftarrow\nabla_\theta f_{\theta_m}(z_m)$, $g_m\leftarrow g_m+B^t\epsilon$, $\epsilon\leftarrow A^t\epsilon$; finally $g_m\leftarrow g_m/K$. (Week 4 slides)`,
  R`<b>Computational efficiency.</b> Evaluate $B_0^tA_1^t\cdots A_{M-1}^t\epsilon$ right-to-left: each step is a matrix-vector product, $O(N^2K)$ total; left-to-right forms dense matrix-matrix products, $O(N^3K)$. This fast algorithm is also called <em>adjoint differentiation</em>. The stored forward states $z_m$ are needed to evaluate $A_m,B_m$.`,
  R`<b>DAG networks.</b> A directed acyclic graph has no loops, always terminates, and is still a DAG when the arrows are reversed. A node with fan-in 2: $x=f_{n,\theta}(y_1,y_2)$ with $A_{n,1}=\nabla_{y_1}f_n$, $A_{n,2}=\nabla_{y_2}f_n$, $B_n=\nabla_\theta f_n$. Back-prop sends $\delta_1=A_{n,1}^t\epsilon$, $\delta_2=A_{n,2}^t\epsilon$, $g=B_n^t\epsilon$; where a forward node fans out, the backward contributions <em>add</em>.`,
  R`<b>Two functions per node.</b> Forward $x\leftarrow F(z_1,z_2,\theta)$ and backward $(\delta_1,\delta_2,g)\leftarrow G(\epsilon,z_1,z_2,\theta)$ which multiplies $\epsilon$ by the adjoint gradient. <em>You never need to compute $\nabla f_n^t$ as a matrix</em>; $G$ produces the products directly. This is how PyTorch autograd is organized. (Week 4 slides)`,
  R`<b>Conv layer forward/backward.</b> $F(z,w,b)=\sigma(w*z+b)$, output $(N-k+1)^2\times C_{out}$ for valid boundaries. $G(\epsilon,z,w,b)$ returns $\delta=\nabla_z f^t\epsilon$: reverse the kernel spatially, swap channel roles, convolve with $\nabla\sigma\,\epsilon$ (zero-padded so the result has the size of $z$); $g_b=\sum_{\text{pixels}}\nabla\sigma\,\epsilon$ per output channel; $g_w=\nabla_wf^t\epsilon$: correlation of $\nabla\sigma\,\epsilon$ with $z$, output the size of $w$. (Week 5 slides)`,
  R`<b>Convolution matrices and their transposes.</b> $x=Az$ with $A_{i,j}=w(i-j)$; $A^t_{i,j}=w(j-i)$ is convolution with $w(-n)$. Valid boundary: $A$ is $(N-k+1)\times N$, $A^t$ maps back to $N$ by zero-padding and using the reversed kernel ("full" convolution). Same/circular boundary: $A$ circulant, $A^t$ circulant with reversed kernel. Convolution is commutative, correlation is not.`,
  R`<b>Linear vs nonlinear layers.</b> For a purely linear layer $\delta=A^t\epsilon$ does not depend on $z$. For $\sigma(Az+b)$, $\nabla_z=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma'(Az+b))A$ and the backward function needs the stored pre-activation. ReLU makes $\nabla\sigma$ a 0/1 mask; a dead ReLU (input $\le0$ everywhere) zeroes all back-propagated gradients, hence leaky ReLU. (Week 11 slides)`,
  R`<b>General losses.</b> Replace $\epsilon_k=-2(x_k-f)$ by $\epsilon_k=[\nabla_b\rho(a,b)]^t$: MAE $-\mathrm{sign}(x_k-f)$, cross-entropy $-x_k./f$. For a DAG with several outputs each output supplies its own $\epsilon$ and the backward pass is otherwise unchanged. (Week 4 slides)`,
  R`<b>Skip connections in the backward pass.</b> $\hat x=y+f_\theta(y)$ has adjoint $\epsilon\mapsto(I+A^t)\epsilon$, so a chain of residual blocks propagates $g=B^t(I+A_1^t)\cdots(I+A_M^t)\epsilon$; with $\sigma_{\max}\approx1$ the error is not attenuated, which fixes vanishing gradients. Merge (concatenate) connections avoid the information loss of adding, e.g. when $f_\theta(y)=-y$. (Week 11 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/sgd"] = [
  R`<b>Batches and epochs.</b> Partition the training indices $S=\{1,\dots,K\}=\bigcup_{b=1}^BS_b$ into randomized batches of size $K_b=|S_b|$; $\nabla L(\theta;S_b)$ is the gradient on batch $b$. SGD: repeat until converged $\{$ for $b=1..B$: $d\leftarrow-\nabla L(\theta;S_b)$; $\theta\leftarrow\theta+\alpha d^t\}$. One inner iteration is a batch; one full pass through all $B$ batches ($K/K_b$ updates) is an <em>epoch</em>. (Week 9 slides)`,
  R`<b>Statistical model.</b> Assume sampling with replacement. Per-sample gradient $g_k=\nabla L_k(\theta)$; a random draw $G$ has distribution $p(g)=\tfrac1K\sum_k\delta(g-g_k)$ (the histogram), mean $\mu=\bar g=\tfrac1K\sum_kg_k$ (the true full-batch gradient) and covariance $R=\tfrac1K\sum_k(g_k-\bar g)(g_k-\bar g)^t$.`,
  R`<b>Batch gradient as signal plus noise.</b> $\hat g=\tfrac1{K_b}\sum_{i=0}^{K_b-1}g_{k_i}=\bar g+\dfrac{w}{\sqrt{K_b}}$ with $E[w]=0$, $\mathrm{Var}(w)\approx R$; equivalently $E[\hat g]=\bar g$ and $\mathrm{Cov}(\hat g)=R/K_b$. Noise standard deviation $\propto1/\sqrt{K_b}$: doubling the batch divides the noise by $\sqrt2$; quadrupling halves it.`,
  R`<b>Why SGD works.</b> A small-batch gradient is much faster to compute and almost as good as the full gradient: with $K=10{,}000$ and $K_b=32$, one SGD iteration is $\approx312\times$ cheaper than one GD iteration, so per unit of compute you take hundreds of noisy steps instead of one exact step.`,
  R`<b>Exploration vs exploitation.</b> Bumpy loss: the full gradient $-\bar g$ is "very bad and slow" (stuck in a local minimum) while the noisy $-\hat g$ is "better and fast" (noise kicks it out). Smooth loss near the minimum: $-\bar g$ is "perfect but slow", $-\hat g$ "not so good but fast" (hunts around the minimum). So: small batches early to explore, large batches (or small $\alpha$) late to exploit.`,
  R`<b>Batch-size trade-offs.</b> Larger $K_b$: less noise, better local convergence, more parallel hardware use, but slower updates, less exploration, more memory. Smaller $K_b$: faster updates, better exploration and escape from local minima, less memory, but hunts around the minimum. Step size $\alpha$: too large hunts, too small is slow. Patches: smaller patches do <em>not</em> speed training or add data; they may fit GPU cache, and the valid region shrinks for deep CNNs.`,
  R`<b>Momentum pseudo-code.</b> init $v\leftarrow0$; for each batch: $d\leftarrow-\nabla L(\theta;S_b)$; $v\leftarrow\gamma v+\alpha(1-\gamma)d$; $\theta\leftarrow\theta+v^t$. Typical $\gamma=0.9$. $\theta$ is position, $v$ velocity, $1-\gamma$ friction; $\gamma=0$ recovers plain SGD.`,
  R`<b>Momentum as a low-pass filter.</b> For an impulse $d_n=\delta_n$: $\theta_n=\alpha(1-\gamma^{n+1})$ for $n\ge0$, approaching $\alpha$ with time constant $\tau=-1-1/\log\gamma$ ($\gamma=e^{-1/(\tau+1)}$; $\gamma=0.9$ gives $\tau\approx8.5$). For a constant $d$, $v\to\alpha d$: the same asymptotic step as GD, but gradient noise is averaged over $\sim\tau$ batches and consistent directions accumulate. (Week 9 slides)`,
  R`<b>Nesterov momentum.</b> Because of momentum the next point is $\theta+\gamma v^t$ even if $d=0$, so evaluate the gradient there: $d\leftarrow-\nabla L(\theta+\gamma v^t;S_b)$; $v\leftarrow\gamma v+\alpha d$; $\theta\leftarrow\theta+v^t$. "Skate to where the puck is going to be."`,
  R`<b>Preconditioned gradient descent.</b> For any $M\succ0$: $g\leftarrow-\nabla L(\theta)^t$; $d\leftarrow Mg$; $\theta\leftarrow\theta+\alpha d$. Since $\langle d,g\rangle=g^tMg>0$, $d$ is within $90^\circ$ of $g$ and PGD always goes downhill. Choices: $M=B^tB$ or diagonal with positive entries.`,
  R`<b>Adam = momentum + diagonal preconditioning.</b> init $v,r\leftarrow0$, $t\leftarrow0$; per batch: $t\leftarrow t+1$; $d\leftarrow-\nabla L(\theta;S_b)$; $v\leftarrow\beta_1v+(1-\beta_1)d$ (momentum); $r\leftarrow\beta_2r+(1-\beta_2)d^2$ (running mean-square, elementwise); $\hat v\leftarrow v/(1-\beta_1^t)$, $\hat r\leftarrow r/(1-\beta_2^t)$ (bias correction at the start); $\theta\leftarrow\theta+\alpha(\sqrt{\hat r}+\epsilon)^{-1}\hat v$ (elementwise). Typical $\alpha=0.001$, $\beta_1=0.9$, $\beta_2=0.999$, $\epsilon=10^{-8}$. Normalizing by the RMS of each gradient component is a diagonal preconditioner that fixes poor conditioning per coordinate. (Week 9 slides)`,
  R`<b>Learning-rate schedules and epochs.</b> Large $\alpha$ / small batches early (exploration), decay $\alpha$ or grow $K_b$ later (exploitation). Reshuffle batches every epoch. The number of updates per epoch is $K/K_b$, so halving $K_b$ doubles the updates per epoch at the same data cost.`,
  R`<b>Batch normalization interacts with SGD.</b> Per batch compute $\mu_b$, $\sigma_b^2$ of each channel, normalize $\hat z=(z-\mu_b)/\sqrt{\sigma_b^2+\epsilon}$, output $y=\gamma\hat z+\beta$; running estimates $\mu_r\leftarrow\alpha\mu_r+(1-\alpha)\mu_b$ (momentum $\alpha\approx0.99$) are saved for inference. The batch statistics are not updated by back-prop and vary randomly with each batch, adding multiplicative noise. (Week 11 slides)`,
  R`<b>Exam phrasing to expect.</b> "Define an epoch"; "mean and covariance of the batch gradient"; "by what factor does the noise std change if $K_b$ doubles" ($1/\sqrt2$); "advantages of small vs large batches"; "write the momentum update and its steady state" ($v\to\alpha d$); "what is $\gamma=0$" (plain SGD); "what does Adam's $r$ estimate" (average squared gradient).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/gan"] = [
  R`<b>Bayes classifier.</b> Two hypotheses with priors $\pi_0,\pi_1$ and densities $p_0(y),p_1(y)$. Posterior $P\{C=1|y\}=\dfrac{\pi_1p_1(y)}{\pi_0p_0(y)+\pi_1p_1(y)}=\dfrac{\pi_1R(y)}{\pi_0+\pi_1R(y)}$ with likelihood ratio $R(y)=p_1(y)/p_0(y)$. The Bayes (MAP) classifier picks the class with posterior $>\tfrac12$, i.e. class 1 iff $R(y)>\pi_0/\pi_1$; it minimizes the probability of error. (Week 6 and 14 slides)`,
  R`<b>The GAN discriminator is a Bayes classifier.</b> Flip a fair coin: real $Y\sim p_r$ or fake $\tilde Y\sim p_{\theta_g}$. Joint density $p(c,y)=\tfrac12p_r(y)\,\delta(c=R)+\tfrac12p_{\theta_g}(y)\,\delta(c=F)$. The optimal discriminator is $$f^\ast(y)=P\{C=R\,|\,y\}=\frac{p_r(y)}{p_r(y)+p_{\theta_g}(y)}=\frac1{1+R(y)},\qquad R(y)=\frac{p_{\theta_g}(y)}{p_r(y)}.$$ Classify "real" where $R&lt;1$, "fake" where $R>1$.`,
  R`<b>Likelihood-ratio facts.</b> $R(y)=\tilde p(y)/p(y)$ requires $\tilde p$ absolutely continuous w.r.t. $p$. $E_p[R(Y)]=\int\tilde p=1$. Change of measure: for any $h$, $E[h(\tilde Y)]=E[h(Y)R(Y)]$. Convex combinations of valid ratios are valid ($E=1$, nonnegative); a constant $R\equiv2$ is not. The generator parameter space is $\Omega_g=\{R:\mathbb R^N\to[0,\infty),\ E[R(Y)]=1\}$, the discriminator space $\Omega_d=\{f:\mathbb R^N\to[0,1]\}$. (Week 14 slides)`,
  R`<b>Non-saturating GAN losses.</b> Generator $g(\theta_g,\theta_d)=E[-\log f_{\theta_d}(\tilde Y(\theta_g))]$ (wants fakes called real); discriminator $d(\theta_g,\theta_d)=E[-\log f_{\theta_d}(Y)]+E[-\log(1-f_{\theta_d}(\tilde Y))]$ (cross-entropy on real/fake labels). Reparametrized: $g(R,f)=E[-R(Y)\log f(Y)]$, $d(R,f)=E[-\log f(Y)]+E[-R(Y)\log(1-f(Y))]$.`,
  R`<b>Nash equilibrium.</b> $\theta_g^\ast=\arg\min_{\theta_g}g(\theta_g,\theta_d^\ast)$ and $\theta_d^\ast=\arg\min_{\theta_d}d(\theta_g^\ast,\theta_d)$ simultaneously: neither player can improve unilaterally. Writing $D(\theta_g)=\arg\min_{\theta_d}d(\theta_g,\theta_d)$ (best discriminator for each generator), the generator solves $\min_{\theta_g}g(\theta_g,D(\theta_g))$. A zero-sum game has $g=-d$ (chess, go); the non-saturating GAN is not zero-sum.`,
  R`<b>At convergence.</b> $R^\ast(y)=1$ (generated distribution $=$ reference), $f^\ast(y)=\tfrac1{1+R}=\tfrac12$ (discriminator cannot tell), generator loss $g=-\log\tfrac12=\log2\approx0.693$, discriminator loss $d=-2\log\tfrac12=2\log2\approx1.386$. On a training plot, generator loss and half the discriminator loss should both settle near $0.693$; the same picture can also come from a discriminator with insufficient capacity.`,
  R`<b>Algorithm 1, alternating minimization, fails.</b> $\hat f\leftarrow\arg\min_fd(\hat R,f)=1/(1+\hat R)$; $\hat R\leftarrow\arg\min_Rg(R,\hat f)$ puts all mass on $y_o=\arg\max_yf(y)=\arg\min_yR(y)$, a delta function. This is <em>mode collapse</em>: the generator only produces the single sample the discriminator likes best ("too good to be true").`,
  R`<b>Algorithm 2, generator-loss gradient descent (GLGD).</b> Define $f^\ast(R)=\arg\min_fd(R,f)$ and the single loss $L(R)=g(R,f^\ast(R))$; iterate $\{f^\ast\leftarrow1/(1+R);\ R\leftarrow R-\alpha P_o\nabla_Rg(R,f^\ast)\}$ with $P_o$ the projection onto $\Omega_g$, $P_o d(y)=d(y)-E[d(Y)]$ (in practice not an issue). Intuition: minimize the discriminator a lot for each generator step.`,
  R`<b>Convergence theorem for GLGD.</b> For the non-saturating GAN, $\nabla_Rg(R,f^\ast)=\nabla_RC(R)$ with $C(R)=E[(1+R(Y))\log(1+R(Y))]$. Since $h(x)=(1+x)\log(1+x)$ has $h''(x)=1/(1+x)>0$, $h$ is strictly convex on $\mathbb R^+$, so $C$ has a unique global minimum on $\Omega_g$ at $R\equiv1$ (Jensen: $E[h(R)]\ge h(E[R])=h(1)$ with equality iff $R$ is constant). GLGD is gradient descent on $C(R)$; convergence tends to be slow. Equivalently $C(R)=2\,KL((p_R+p_r)/2\,\|\,p_r)$. (Week 14 slides)`,
  R`<b>Divergences to know.</b> $KL(p_a\|p_b)=E_a[\log p_a(X)-\log p_b(X)]\ge0$; $JSD(p_a\|p_b)=\tfrac12KL(p_a\|\bar p)+\tfrac12KL(p_b\|\bar p)$ with $\bar p=\tfrac12(p_a+p_b)$. With $p_R=Rp_r$ and the change of measure: $KL(p_R\|p_r)=E[\log R(\tilde Y)]=E[R(Y)\log R(Y)]$ and $2KL(\tfrac{p_R+p_r}2\|p_r)=E[(1+R(Y))\log(1+R(Y))]$. Useful identity (proved on the slides): $KL(\bar p\|p_2)=KL(p_1\|p_2)-2JSD(p_1\|p_2)$ with $\bar p=\tfrac12(p_1+p_2)$. Arjovsky-Bottou: $P_o\nabla_Rg=\nabla_R[KL(p_R\|p_r)-2JSD(p_R\|p_r)]$.`,
  R`<b>Practical algorithm and failure mode.</b> Repeat $\{$ for $N_d$ iterations: new batch, $\theta_d\leftarrow\theta_d-\beta\nabla_{\theta_d}d$; new batch, $\theta_g\leftarrow\theta_g-\alpha\nabla_{\theta_g}g\}$. Mode collapse shows as the discriminator loss dropping toward 0 while the generator loss climbs (discriminator dominates); causes: discriminator overfitting, too few discriminator updates, insufficient generator capacity.`,
  R`<b>Conditional GAN.</b> Generate from $p(y|x)$: generator $\tilde y_k=h_{\theta_g}(x_k,z_k)$ with latent noise $z_k$; discriminator sees pairs $(y_k,x_k)$ labeled 1 and $(\tilde y_k,x_k)$ labeled 0; $d=\mathrm{CE}(p_k,1)+\mathrm{CE}(\tilde p_k,0)$, $g=\mathrm{CE}(\tilde p_k,1)$. (Week 14 slides)`,
  R`<b>Wasserstein GAN.</b> The Bayes discriminator is too sensitive/nonlinear and non-overlapping supports give vanishing gradients. Replace it by the earth-mover distance $W(p_r\|p_R)=\inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\int\|x-y\|\gamma(x,y)\,dx\,dy=\sup_{\|f\|_L\le1}\{E[f(Y)]-E[f(\tilde Y)]\}$ (Kantorovich-Rubinstein). Losses $g_w=E[-f(\tilde Y)]$, $d_w=E[f(\tilde Y)]-E[f(Y)]$ over 1-Lipschitz $f$, enforced by clipping discriminator weights (a form of regularization); $\nabla_Rg_w(R,f^\ast)=\nabla_RW(p_r\|p_R)$.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/seq"] = [
  R`<b>Autoregressive factorization.</b> Chain rule for a sequence $y_n=(x_0,\dots,x_n)$: $p_n(y_n)=g_n(x_n|y_{n-1})\,p_{n-1}(y_{n-1})=\prod_{i=0}^ng_i(x_i|y_{i-1})$ with $y_{-1}=\emptyset$. Sampling procedure: for $i=0,1,\dots$ draw $X_i\sim g_i(\cdot|Y_{i-1})$ and append. This is the "autoregressive models: $Y_n\sim p(y_n|y_{n-1})$" branch of the generative-model taxonomy. (Week 15 slides)`,
  R`<b>How an LLM generates text.</b> Input $=$ prompt; repeat until output $=$ EOS: output $\leftarrow$ LLM(input) gives token probabilities at the last position; pick a token (MAP decode $=\arg\max$, or sample the softmax by inverse-CDF); input $\leftarrow$ [input, output]; if length exceeds the context window drop the first token. Masked attention makes the model an autoregressive (causal) model of language. (Week 12 slides)`,
  R`<b>Single-head attention.</b> $\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K,V)=\mathrm{softmax}\!\left(\dfrac{QK^t}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)V$ with $Q\in\mathbb R^{n\times d_m}$ (queries, $n$ inputs), $K,V\in\mathbb R^{m\times d_m}$ ($m$ inputs), learned projections $W^Q,W^K\in\mathbb R^{d_m\times d_k}$, $W^V\in\mathbb R^{d_m\times d_v}$; scores $S=QK^t$, row-wise softmax, output $O=SV\in\mathbb R^{n\times d_v}$. Intuition: find the best-matching key for each query and return the corresponding value (a soft lookup table).`,
  R`<b>Self-attention and multi-head.</b> Self-attention: $Q=K=V$ (same input), so $S$ is square $n\times n$. Multi-head: $h$ single-head blocks with their own $W_i^Q,W_i^K,W_i^V$, outputs concatenated and mixed by $W^O\in\mathbb R^{hd_v\times d_m}$; $O$ has the same shape as $Q$, $n$ can change from call to call, $d_m$ sets the model order.`,
  R`<b>Self-attention as denoising (the 2026 problem).</b> Rows of $Q$ i.i.d. $N(0,\sigma^2I_p)$-like: $\|a\|^2\approx p\sigma^2$, $\langle a,b\rangle\approx0$ for unrelated rows. For rows $a,a,b,b$, $QQ^t/\sqrt p$ has $\approx\sqrt p\,\sigma^2$ on the two $2\times2$ diagonal blocks and $\approx0$ elsewhere; for large $p$ the row softmax becomes $\tfrac12$ on the matching pair and $0$ off it, so $Z=\mathrm{softmax}(QQ^t/\sqrt p)Q$ averages each token with the tokens that resemble it: attention <em>denoises by averaging similar tokens</em>, exactly like the non-local-means filter ($w_{i,j}=\exp(-\|p_i-p_j\|^2)$ normalized is a softmax, $\hat p_i=\sum_j\bar w_{i,j}p_j$ is the $SV$ product).`,
  R`<b>Masked (causal) attention.</b> Add $-\infty$ to the upper-triangular part of the score matrix, $S=QK^t-\infty\,\delta(j>i)$, so after the softmax position $i$ attends only to positions $\le i$. Only used with self-attention ($n=m$). It enforces causality, is the basis of autoregressive models and LLMs, and has largely replaced RNNs. With input $q_0=$BOS$,a,b,c,d,e$ the outputs $o_0..o_4$ predict $a..e$ and $o_5$ predicts the next symbol. (Week 12 slides)`,
  R`<b>Cost.</b> The score matrix is $P\times P$ for a context window of $P$ tokens, so compute and memory scale as $O(P^2d)$; this is why the context window is limited and why "if it is too long, then the computation for the transformer will be too large."`,
  R`<b>Normalization and the transformer layer.</b> Layer norm: per row, $\mu_i=\tfrac1d\sum_jQ_{i,j}$, $\sigma_i^2=\tfrac1d\sum_j(Q_{i,j}-\mu_i)^2$, $O_{i,j}=(Q_{i,j}-\mu_i)/\sigma_i$; RMS norm skips the mean. These are <em>not</em> learned. A modern (pre-norm) transformer layer: $Q\to$ Norm $\to$ MHA $\to$ add skip $\to$ Norm $\to$ MLP $\to$ add skip; the MLP acts per token ($\equiv$ a $1\times1$ CNN); input and output shapes are equal. A transformer stacks $N_L$ such layers.`,
  R`<b>Tokens, embedding, positional encoding.</b> Tokenizer maps character strings to integers in a vocabulary of size $|V|\sim50$K (byte-pair encoding); one-hot then a learned $E\in\mathbb R^{|V|\times d_m}$ gives token vectors; the decoder reverses with $E^t$ (often the same matrix) and a softmax to token probabilities. Positional encoding is <em>added</em> because attention is permutation invariant: sinusoidal $P(t,2i)=\sin(t/\lambda_i)$, $P(t,2i+1)=\cos(t/\lambda_i)$, $\lambda_i=N^{2i/d}$, or learned. (Week 11-12 slides)`,
  R`<b>Generative pre-training.</b> Unlabeled text supplies both input and target: input $x_0..x_{P-1}$, target the same sequence shifted one token left, $x_1..x_P$ with EOS appended at document ends. Loss: cross-entropy between the softmax prediction at each position and the one-hot next token (maximum likelihood of the next token), computed for all positions in parallel thanks to masking.`,
  R`<b>Model sizes to have a feel for.</b> GPT-2 small: $N_L=12$, $d_m=768$, $h=12$, $d_k=64$, $d_{mlp}=3072$, context 1024, 117M parameters. LLaMA-2 70B: $N_L=80$, $d_m=8192$, $h=64$, $d_k=128$, context 4096. Parameters per layer scale like $\sim12d_m^2$ (attention $4d_m^2$ plus MLP $8d_m^2$).`,
  R`<b>Vision transformer.</b> Split a $224\times224\times3$ image into $14\times14=196$ patches of $16\times16$, flatten and linearly project each to $768$ dims, add learned positional encodings, optionally prepend a class token ($197\times768$), run a transformer encoder, MLP head on the class token. (Week 12 slides)`,
  R`<b>RNNs for contrast.</b> $(y_t,s_t)=f_\theta(x_t,s_{t-1})$ with shared $\theta$ ("unrolling the loop"); back-prop iterates in time and gradients vanish over long horizons. LSTM keeps a cell state $C_t=f_t\circ C_{t-1}+i_t\circ\tilde C_t$ (forget/input gates via sigmoids, Hadamard products act like skip connections), $h_t=o_t\circ\tanh C_t$; GRU merges cell and hidden states with an update gate $h_t=(1-z_t)\circ h_{t-1}+z_t\circ\tilde h_t$. (Week 13 slides)`,
  R`<b>Autoencoders and latent generators.</b> Encoder $z=f_{\theta_e}(y)$, decoder $\hat y=g_{\theta_d}(z)$, unsupervised loss $\sum_k\|y_k-g(f(y_k))\|^2$. The decoder driven by a random $z$ is a generator (VAE); natural images are extremely sparse in $\mathbb R^N$ (a uniform white-noise image never looks natural; all images ever seen fit in $\sim93$ bits of index), which is why low-dimensional latents work. (Week 13 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/mapreg"] = [
  R`<b>Frequentist training model.</b> $\theta$ is a deterministic unknown, training data $(X,Y)\sim p_\theta(x,y)$, estimator $\hat\theta=T(X,Y)$. ML: $\hat\theta=\arg\max_\theta p_\theta(X,Y)=\arg\min_\theta\{-\log p_\theta(X,Y)\}$, usually solved via $\nabla_\theta\log p_\theta=0$. Since $\log p_\theta(x,y)=\log p_\theta(x|y)+\log p(y)$, only the conditional term depends on $\theta$. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>MSE loss is ML under Gaussian noise.</b> $X_k=f_\theta(Y_k)+W_k$, $W_k\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, i.i.d. pairs: $p_\theta(x_k|y_k)=(2\pi\sigma^2)^{-p/2}\exp\{-\tfrac1{2\sigma^2}\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2\}$, so $$\hat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\sum_k\Big[\tfrac1{2\sigma^2}\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2+\tfrac p2\log(2\pi\sigma^2)\Big]=\arg\min_\theta\tfrac1K\sum_k\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2=\arg\min_\theta L_{MSE}(\theta).$$ Laplacian noise gives the MAE ($L_1$) loss instead.`,
  R`<b>Cross-entropy is ML for classification.</b> One-hot $x_k$ and $p_\theta(x_k|y_k)=\prod_m[f_\theta(y_k)]_m^{x_{k,m}}$ give $-\log p_\theta(x|y)=\sum_k\rho_{CE}(x_k,f_\theta(y_k))=K\,L_{CE}(\theta)$. Rule: pick the loss that is the negative log-likelihood of your noise/label model.`,
  R`<b>Bayesian model of training.</b> Treat $\theta$ as random with prior $p(\theta)$: $p(x,y,\theta)=p(\theta)\prod_kp(x_k|y_k,\theta)p(y_k)$. MAP: $\hat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\{-\log p_\theta(x|y)-\log p(\theta)\}$; MMSE would be $E[\theta|x,y]$ (rarely used for weights).`,
  R`<b>Laplacian prior gives $L_1$ regularization.</b> $p(\theta)\propto\exp\{-\|\theta\|_1/\sigma\}$ with the Gaussian likelihood: $\hat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\tfrac1{2\sigma_w^2}\sum_k\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2+\tfrac1\sigma\|\theta\|_1=\arg\min_\theta\sum_k\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2+\beta\|\theta\|_1$ with $\beta=2\sigma_w^2/\sigma$. Noisier data or a tighter prior increases $\beta$. A Gaussian prior $\exp\{-\|\theta\|^2/2\sigma^2\}$ gives $L_2$ weight decay with $\beta=\sigma_w^2/\sigma^2$.`,
  R`<b>Regularized ML in general.</b> $\hat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\{-\log p_\theta(x,y)+\beta S(\theta)\}$, i.e. modified loss $\tilde L=L+\beta S$. Choices $S(\theta)=-\log p(\theta)$, $\|\theta\|_1$, $\|\theta\|_2^2$. Interpretable as MAP with $p(\theta)=\tfrac1z\exp\{-\tfrac\beta\sigma S(\theta)\}$. $L_2$ reduces weight amplitudes (Gaussian-like); $L_1$ encourages exact zeros (Laplacian-like), effectively reducing model order. (Week 6-7 slides)`,
  R`<b>Implementing each in standard software.</b> ML with Gaussian noise: MSELoss on $f_\theta(y_k)$ vs $x_k$. ML for classes: CrossEntropyLoss on logits. MAP with $L_2$: add $\beta\|\theta\|^2$ to the loss or use the optimizer's weight_decay. MAP with $L_1$: add $\beta\sum|\theta_i|$ explicitly (autograd handles the sign). Data augmentation, dropout and early stopping are further implicit priors.`,
  R`<b>ML vs MAP: when to use which.</b> ML: no prior needed, (mostly) unbiased, asymptotically efficient (Cramer-Rao), best with lots of high-quality data; overfits when $P\gg N_xK$. MAP: lower variance when the prior is right, better with few or poor data and many parameters, but biased and needs a prior plus a hyperparameter $\beta$ chosen on validation data.`,
  R`<b>Bias-variance framing.</b> Frequentist $\mathrm{MSE}=\mathrm{Var}+\mathrm{bias}^2$ with $\mathrm{bias}=E[\hat\theta|\theta]-\theta$. Regularization introduces bias to reduce variance; bias is "usually worse than variance" (blur vs noise), yet as bias $\to0$ variance $\to\infty$ with finite data. Use regularization if training error $\ll$ validation error. (Week 6 slides)`,
  R`<b>Inference is Bayesian, training is frequentist.</b> Inference estimates $\hat x=f_\theta(y)$ from $y$: typically Bayesian (MMSE $E[X|Y]$ or MAP), always biased. Training estimates $\hat\theta$ from $\{(x_k,y_k)\}$: typically frequentist, unbiased unless regularized. The MSE-trained network approximates the MMSE estimator $E[X|Y]$.`,
  R`<b>The MAP denoising example.</b> $y=x+w$, $w\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, prior $p(x)$: $\hat x_{MAP}=\arg\min_x\tfrac1{2\sigma^2}\|y-x\|^2-\log p(x)$, a proximal map of the prior energy $u(x)=-\log p(x)$. Prior information "fills the space but has thickness": the data manifold plus a prior manifold makes ill-posed inverse problems solvable. (Week 6 and 15 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/precond"] = [
  R`<b>Least-squares objective.</b> $f(x)=\tfrac12\|y-Ax\|^2=\tfrac12(y-Ax)^t(y-Ax)$. Gradient (as a column) $\nabla f(x)=-A^t(y-Ax)=A^tAx-A^ty$; descent direction $d=-\nabla f=A^t(y-Ax)$, the adjoint applied to the residual. Hessian $\nabla^2f=A^tA$, symmetric and non-negative definite, positive definite iff $A$ has full column rank. (The posted 2026 key wrote $AA^t$; the correct Hessian is $A^tA$.)`,
  R`<b>Convexity.</b> $f$ is a convex function of a linear transform ($\|\cdot\|^2$ composed with $x\mapsto y-Ax$), hence convex; strictly convex when $A^tA\succ0$, so $\nabla f=0$ characterizes the unique global minimum: the normal equations $A^tAx=A^ty$. (Week 3 slides)`,
  R`<b>Plain gradient step decreases $f$.</b> First-order expansion $f(x+\alpha d)=f(x)+\alpha\nabla f(x)^td+O(\alpha^2)=f(x)-\alpha\|d\|^2+O(\alpha^2)$, so for small $\alpha>0$ and $d\ne0$, $f(x+\alpha d)&lt;f(x)$. Exactly, for a quadratic, $f(x+\alpha d)=f(x)-\alpha\|d\|^2+\tfrac{\alpha^2}2d^tA^tAd$, which is below $f(x)$ iff $\alpha&lt;2\|d\|^2/\|Ad\|^2$.`,
  R`<b>Preconditioned step decreases $f$.</b> For any $M\succ0$, $\tilde d=Md$ satisfies $\nabla f^t\tilde d=-d^tMd&lt;0$ (a positive-definite quadratic form), so $f(x+\alpha Md)\approx f(x)-\alpha\,d^tMd&lt;f(x)$ for small $\alpha$: the angle between $Md$ and $d$ is less than $90^\circ$ and PGD always goes downhill. Algorithm: $g\leftarrow-\nabla f^t$; $d\leftarrow Mg$; $x\leftarrow x+\alpha d$. (Week 9 slides)`,
  R`<b>Newton step $=$ least-squares solution in one iteration.</b> With $M=(\nabla^2f)^{-1}=(A^tA)^{-1}$ and $\alpha=1$: $x\leftarrow x+(A^tA)^{-1}A^t(y-Ax)=x+(A^tA)^{-1}A^ty-x=(A^tA)^{-1}A^ty$, the normal-equation solution, independent of the starting point. Newton "spheres the ellipse": the effective matrix $MH=I$ has condition number 1.`,
  R`<b>Exact line search on a quadratic.</b> Along $d$, $\phi(\alpha)=f(x+\alpha d)$ is quadratic with $\phi'(0)=-\|d\|^2$ and $\phi''=d^tA^tAd$, so $\alpha^\ast=\dfrac{d^td}{d^tA^tAd}$. Steepest descent with this $\alpha$ zig-zags: consecutive directions are orthogonal.`,
  R`<b>Why GD on least squares can be slow.</b> $x_{k+1}-x^\ast=(I-\alpha A^tA)(x_k-x^\ast)$; in the eigenbasis of $A^tA$ each mode decays as $(1-\alpha\lambda_i)^k$, stable iff $\alpha&lt;2/\lambda_{\max}$, and the slowest mode decays at $1-\alpha\lambda_{\min}\approx1-2/\kappa$ with $\kappa=\lambda_{\max}/\lambda_{\min}=\sigma_{\max}(A)^2/\sigma_{\min}(A)^2$. Elongated contours, gradient across the valley. (Week 2 slides)`,
  R`<b>Choosing $M$ in practice.</b> $M=H^{-1}$ (Newton) is exact but needs a $P\times P$ solve: too much computation and too hard to implement for deep nets. Cheap alternatives: $M=B^tB$ for any full-rank $B$; diagonal $M=\mathrm{diag}(a_i)$, $a_i>0$, e.g. $a_i=1/H_{ii}$ (exact if $H$ is diagonal); Adam's $(\sqrt{\hat r}+\epsilon)^{-1}$, a diagonal preconditioner estimated from gradient statistics; quasi-Newton (L-BFGS) builds a low-rank $M$ from gradient differences.`,
  R`<b>Newton for general smooth $f$.</b> $x\leftarrow x-(\nabla^2f)^{-1}\nabla f^t$ minimizes the local quadratic model; converges quadratically near a minimum with $\nabla^2f\succ0$, but away from it the Hessian may be indefinite (saddles) and the step may go uphill, so damping ($\alpha&lt;1$) or a positive-definite modification is needed.`,
  R`<b>Connections.</b> $d=A^t(y-Ax)$ is exactly the back-propagated error of an MSE loss through the linear layer $A$ ($\epsilon=-2(y-Ax)$ up to scale, $A^t\epsilon$). Preconditioning, momentum and Adam are the three practical answers to ill-conditioning listed on the Week 2 slide; vanishing gradients are the deep-network version of a huge condition number. (Week 2, 9, 11 slides)`,
  R`<b>Exam checklist.</b> (i) $\nabla f=-A^t(y-Ax)$; (ii) show $f(x+\alpha d)&lt;f(x)$ via $\nabla f^td=-\|d\|^2&lt;0$; (iii) show $f(x+\alpha Md)&lt;f(x)$ via $d^tMd>0$; (iv) Hessian $A^tA$; (v) one step with $M=(A^tA)^{-1}$, $\alpha=1$ lands on $(A^tA)^{-1}A^ty$; (vi) state when $A^tA$ is invertible (full column rank, $A$ tall with independent columns).`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/genrv"] = [
  R`<b>Inference vs generation.</b> Inference model: learn a function $\hat x=f_\theta(y)$ (predict an unknown). Generative model: learn to sample $Y\sim p_\theta(y)$ (generate random vectors). Key issues: how to learn the distribution from samples, and how to generate random vectors with a desired distribution. (Week 15 slides)`,
  R`<b>Taxonomy of generative methods.</b> <em>Direct generation</em> $Y=F_Y^{-1}(U)$ (inverse CDF). <em>Generator methods</em> $Y=h_\theta(Z)$ with $Z$ simple noise (VAEs, GANs). <em>Autoregressive</em> $Y_n\sim p(y_n|y_{n-1})$ (transformers). <em>Monte Carlo / Gibbs</em> $p(y)=\tfrac1Z\exp\{-u(y)\}$ sampled by Metropolis-Hastings, score matching and generative diffusion ($-\nabla_yu\approx s_\theta$). All of them ultimately consume uniform or Gaussian pseudo-random numbers.`,
  R`<b>CDF facts.</b> $F(x)=P\{X\le x\}$ is non-decreasing, right-continuous, $F(-\infty)=0$, $F(\infty)=1$, and $P\{a&lt;X\le b\}=F(b)-F(a)$. If $F$ is continuous and <em>strictly increasing</em> then its range is the open interval $(0,1)$ (the endpoints are limits, never attained, e.g. the Gaussian), it is one-to-one, and $F^{-1}:(0,1)\to\mathbb R$ exists with $F(F^{-1}(u))=u$ and $F^{-1}(F(x))=x$.`,
  R`<b>Inverse-CDF theorem and proof.</b> Let $U\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)$ and $X=F^{-1}(U)$. Then for any $\lambda$, $$P\{X\le\lambda\}=P\{F^{-1}(U)\le\lambda\}=P\{U\le F(\lambda)\}=F(\lambda),$$ using that $F$ is increasing (so $F^{-1}(u)\le\lambda\iff u\le F(\lambda)$) and $P\{U\le t\}=t$ on $[0,1]$. Hence $X$ has CDF $F$. Conversely $F(X)\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)$ for continuous $F$.`,
  R`<b>Why it matters.</b> A computer only produces uniform pseudo-random numbers; the inverse CDF converts them into samples from <em>any</em> distribution with a computable $F^{-1}$. Every generative model is a fancy $F^{-1}$: a network that maps simple noise to samples from the empirical distribution.`,
  R`<b>Worked examples.</b> Exponential with mean $\mu$: $F(x)=1-e^{-x/\mu}$, $X=-\mu\ln(1-U)$ (or $-\mu\ln U$). Cauchy: $X=\tan(\pi(U-\tfrac12))$. Uniform on $(a,b)$: $X=a+(b-a)U$. Discrete $p_i$: form cumulative sums $c_i=\sum_{j\le i}p_j$ and return the smallest $i$ with $U\le c_i$ (this is how a softmax output is sampled). Gaussian: no closed-form $F^{-1}$; use Box-Muller $X=\sqrt{-2\ln U_1}\cos(2\pi U_2)$; $N(\mu,R)$ via $\mu+E\Lambda^{1/2}W$ with $R=E\Lambda E^t$.`,
  R`<b>Generalized inverse.</b> When $F$ has flat parts or jumps, define $F^{-1}(u)=\min\{x:F(x)\ge u\}$; the same proof goes through and yields discrete or mixed distributions. Jumps in $F$ correspond to atoms; flat parts correspond to gaps in the support.`,
  R`<b>Gibbs distributions and MCMC.</b> Any $p(x)>0$ can be written $p(x)=\tfrac1z\exp\{-u(x)\}$ with energy $u=-\log p$ and (usually intractable) partition function $z$. Metropolis: propose $W\sim q(w|X_n)$ (symmetric), $\Delta E=u(W)-u(X_n)$, accept with probability $\min\{e^{-\Delta E},1\}$; Hastings adds the ratio $q(X_n|W)/q(W|X_n)$. The chain is reversible and ergodic with stationary distribution $p$: a way to sample any Gibbs distribution, at the cost of many iterations. (Week 15 slides)`,
  R`<b>Score and Tweedie.</b> Score $s(x)=\nabla_x\log p(x)=-\nabla_xu(x)$. For $\tilde X=X+\sigma W$, $W\sim N(0,I)$, Tweedie's formula gives $E[X|\tilde X]=\tilde X+\sigma^2s_\sigma(\tilde X)$: an MMSE denoiser <em>is</em> a scaled score of the noisy distribution, and $\sigma s_\sigma$ estimates the negative noise (residual training). Denoising score matching trains $s_\theta$ with $\tfrac1{2\sigma^2}\|(x_k-\tilde x_k)/\sigma-\sigma s_\theta(\tilde x_k)\|^2$-type losses without knowing $u$.`,
  R`<b>Generative plug-and-play (GPnP) sampler.</b> Repeat $\{X\leftarrow(1-r\beta)X+r\beta\,\mathrm{Denoise}(X;\sigma)+\sqrt{\beta}\sigma\,\mathrm{RandN}(0,I);\ X\leftarrow\tilde F_f(X)+\sqrt\beta\sigma\,\mathrm{RandN}(0,I);\ \sigma\leftarrow\mathrm{Reduce}(\sigma)\}$ with $\beta\approx\tfrac12$, $r\approx1.3$: alternate a prior proximal generator (a denoiser plus noise) and a forward-model proximal generator, decreasing $\sigma$ from large (explore) to small (exploit). It is a Markov chain whose stationary distribution is the posterior with a Gaussian-blurred prior. "If the data doesn't constrain your problem, the prior will take over." (Week 15 slides)`,
  R`<b>Randomness inside networks.</b> Dropout left on at inference turns $f_\theta(y)$ into a random vector; batch norm injects multiplicative batch noise; GANs and VAEs feed $Z\sim N(0,I)$ (generated via Box-Muller from uniforms) into a decoder; autoregressive models sample each token by inverse CDF on the softmax's cumulative probabilities. (Week 7, 13, 14 slides)`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/cnn2"] = [
  R`<b>Valid convolution size rule.</b> With a $k\times k$ kernel and valid boundary each spatial dimension shrinks by $k-1$: $N_{out}=N_{in}-k+1$. Two layers: $N_1=N_0-(k-1)$, $N_2=N_1-(k-1)=N_0-2(k-1)$. With "same" (zero-padded) boundaries $N$ is unchanged. (Week 5 slides)`,
  R`<b>Parameter count rule.</b> A conv layer from $C_{in}$ to $C_{out}$ channels with $k\times k$ kernels has $k^2C_{in}C_{out}$ weights plus $C_{out}$ offsets: $\#=k^2C_{in}C_{out}+C_{out}$. Weights are shared over all pixel positions, so the count does not depend on $N$.`,
  R`<b>The 2025 Midterm 2 instance.</b> $128\times128\times1$ input, $5\times5$ kernels, channels $1\to8\to3$, valid: $N_1=124$, $N_2=120$; $w_1$: $5\times5\times1\times8$, $b_1$: $8$, $w_2$: $5\times5\times8\times3$, $b_2$: $3$; parameters $200+8+600+3=811$.`,
  R`<b>Tensor shapes and ranks.</b> Feature tensors $z$: $N\times N\times C$ (rank 3). Kernel $w$: $k\times k\times C_{in}\times C_{out}$ (rank 4). Offset $b$: $C_{out}$ (rank 1). In Einstein notation $x_{j_1,j_2,j_3}=\sigma(w_{j_1,j_2,i,j_3}*z_{j_1,j_2,i}+b_{j_3})$ where the repeated channel index $i$ is summed.`,
  R`<b>Layers with zero parameters.</b> ReLU/leaky ReLU, max and average pooling, stride (decimation), upsampling (pixel replication), flatten, softmax. Batch norm has $2C$ learned parameters ($\gamma,\beta$ per channel) plus non-learned running statistics. A dense head on a flattened $N\times N\times C$ tensor costs $N^2C\cdot N_{out}+N_{out}$.`,
  R`<b>Pooling and stride sizes.</b> $p\times p$ pooling with stride $p$ divides each dimension by $p$; pooling with stride 1 shrinks by $p-1$ (valid) or keeps $N$ (same). Conv with stride $s$: $\lfloor(N-k)/s\rfloor+1$. Upsampling by $L$ multiplies $N$ by $L$.`,
  R`<b>Slide reference example.</b> Five $5\times5$ layers $1\to16\to16\to16\to16\to1$: $416+6416+6416+6416+401=20{,}065$ parameters, all layers same-size $512\times512$. Compare with a dense layer on the same image: $\sim(512^2)^2\approx7\times10^{10}$ weights.`,
  R`<b>Why convolution beats fully connected for images.</b> Far fewer parameters (less data, less compute, less overfitting), spatial invariance (recognition should not depend on object position; denoising on content, not location), local receptive fields growing with depth, and the network designs its own per-pixel feature vectors. Drawbacks: fixed resolution per layer, boundary effects, needs pooling/stride plus a dense head for classification.`,
  R`<b>Backward shapes for the same layer.</b> $G(\epsilon,z,w,b)$ receives $\epsilon$ of the output size $N_{out}\times N_{out}\times C_{out}$ and returns $\delta$ of the input size $N_{in}\times N_{in}\times C_{in}$ (reversed kernel, zero-padded convolution), $g_w$ of the kernel size $k\times k\times C_{in}\times C_{out}$ (correlation of $\epsilon$ with $z$), and $g_b$ of size $C_{out}$ (sum of $\epsilon$ over pixels). (Week 5 slides)`,
  R`<b>Checklist.</b> Shrink by $k-1$ per valid layer; multiply $k^2$ by <em>both</em> channel counts; add one bias per output channel; ignore $N$ for the count; zero for pooling/ReLU; report rank $=$ number of axes; double-check that the second layer's $C_{in}$ equals the first layer's $C_{out}$.`
];

window.CONCEPTS["ece60146/mt2/denoise"] = [
  R`<b>Images are points in a high-dimensional space.</b> An $N\times N$ image is a vector $x\in\mathbb R^{N^2}$; natural images are a random vector $X\sim p(x)$, a "cloud of points". A uniform white-noise image $W_i\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)$ fills the space, yet a typical sample has zero chance of looking natural: $p(x)$ is extremely sparse. Thought experiment: $\le10^{10}$ people $\times10^{18}$ images $=10^{28}$ images ever seen, so all of them can be indexed in $\log_210^{28}\approx93$ bits $&lt;16$ bytes. (Week 13 slides)`,
  R`<b>Dense linear operators are impossible.</b> A general linear map on a 1-megapixel image is an $N^2\times N^2=2^{20}\times2^{20}=2^{40}$-entry matrix; at 4 bytes per float that is $2^{42}$ bytes $\approx4$ TB, so image operators are always structured: convolution (shared $k^2C_{in}C_{out}$ weights), FFTs, sparse or low-rank forms. The same reasoning motivates CNNs over dense layers. (Week 5 slides)`,
  R`<b>Marginal vs conditional pixel densities.</b> Marginal $p_i(x_i)=\int p(x)\prod_{j\ne i}dx_j$ is broad: a pixel by itself can take almost any value. Conditional $p(x_i|x_{j\ne i})=p(x)/\int p(x)\,dx_i$ is narrow: given its neighbors a pixel is nearly determined. On an exam plot, the narrow curve is the conditional. This is exactly the structure a denoiser exploits.`,
  R`<b>Denoising as the simplest inverse problem.</b> Forward model $Y=X+W$, $W\sim N(0,\sigma^2I)$, so $E[W_iW_j]=\sigma^2\delta(i-j)$ (white, uncorrelated pixels). Loss $L(\theta)=\tfrac1K\sum_k\|x_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2$; $\hat\theta$ is the ML estimate of the parameters and $\hat x=f_{\hat\theta}(y)$ approximates the MMSE estimate $E[X|Y]$. Training pairs are made by taking clean images $x_k$ typical of the target domain (so the network learns the right prior) and adding synthetic noise. (Week 11 slides)`,
  R`<b>Predict the noise: residual learning.</b> $\hat x=E[X|Y]=E[Y-W|Y]=Y-E[W|Y]=Y-f_\theta(Y)$ where $\hat w=f_\theta(Y)$ estimates the noise. Train with $\|w_k-f_\theta(y_k)\|^2$ and output $y-f_\theta(y)$. Why: the noise is "smaller" (small dynamic range, zero mean) and easier to estimate than the image, and $y-f_\theta(y)$ is a skip connection that fixes vanishing gradients. DnCNN introduced this; DN-ResNet, DRUNet follow it.`,
  R`<b>Skip connections and the adjoint.</b> $\hat x=y+f_\theta(y)$ has adjoint gradient $(I+A^t)$, so deep stacks give $g=B^t(I+A_1^t)\cdots(I+A_M^t)\epsilon$ and the gradient no longer shrinks geometrically. Merge (concatenation) connections avoid the information loss of pure addition (e.g. when $f_\theta(y)=-y$); U-Net uses them across scales, "much like a wavelet transform but nonlinear," and captures long-distance dependencies. (Week 11 slides)`,
  R`<b>Batch normalization in denoisers.</b> Per channel, per batch: $\hat z=(z-\mu_b)/\sqrt{\sigma_b^2+\epsilon}$, $y=\gamma\hat z+\beta$; running $\mu_r,\sigma_r^2$ (momentum $\approx0.99$) are used at inference. Works well for residual estimation (outputs small, zero-mean) but adds multiplicative noise; do not use it when outputs must be large and precise, or freeze its adaptation for final training.`,
  R`<b>Architectures.</b> DnCNN: fixed-resolution $3\times3$ conv+BN+ReLU stack (~15-20 layers) predicting the residual; limited to local dependencies and trained for one noise level. DN-ResNet: residual blocks. DRUNet: U-Net with residual encoding and the noise level $\sigma$ supplied as an extra input channel (positional encoding of $\sigma$ in DDPM). Plug-and-play: iterate the denoiser with a physics forward model via proximal maps.`,
  R`<b>Denoisers are scores (Tweedie).</b> With $\tilde X=X+\sigma W$ and $s_\sigma(\tilde x)=\nabla\log p_\sigma(\tilde x)$: $E[X|\tilde X]=\tilde X+\sigma^2s_\sigma(\tilde X)$, equivalently $s_\sigma(\tilde x)=\tfrac1{\sigma^2}(\mathrm{Denoise}(\tilde x;\sigma)-\tilde x)$. So the MMSE denoiser adds the scaled score of the <em>noisy</em> distribution, $\sigma s_\sigma$ is the negative-noise estimate, i.e. residual training, and as $\sigma\to0$ the score approaches that of the clean images. The denoiser moves samples toward higher probability. (Week 15 slides)`,
  R`<b>Denoising score matching.</b> Vincent's theorem: $E\tfrac12\|s_\sigma(\tilde X)-s_\theta(\tilde X)\|^2=E\tfrac12\|\nabla_{\tilde x}\log q_\sigma(\tilde X|X)-s_\theta(\tilde X)\|^2+C$; with Gaussian noise $\nabla_{\tilde x}\log q_\sigma=(x-\tilde x)/\sigma^2$, so the practical loss is $\sum_k\tfrac12\|\tfrac1{\sigma^2}(x_k-\tilde x_k)-s_\theta(\tilde x_k)\|^2$ over noisy/clean pairs: you learn the score of the data without ever knowing $u(x)$. Large $\sigma$ explores low-probability regions (used early), small $\sigma$ refines (used late).`,
  R`<b>Denoisers as priors for any inverse problem.</b> Plug-and-play and generative PnP alternate a prior step (denoiser, possibly with added noise) and a forward-model step (proximal map / projection onto the sensor manifold): $X\leftarrow(1-r\beta)X+r\beta\,\mathrm{Denoise}(X;\sigma)+\sqrt\beta\sigma W$, $X\leftarrow\tilde F_f(X)+\sqrt\beta\sigma W$, reduce $\sigma$. The stationary distribution is the posterior with a Gaussian-blurred prior; the same denoiser serves inpainting, tomography, deblurring. (Week 15 slides)`,
  R`<b>Exam-ready numbers and statements.</b> $2^{42}$ bytes for a dense $1024^2\times1024^2$ float matrix; $E[W_iW_j]=\sigma^2\delta(i-j)$; loss $\|W_k-f_\theta(Y_k)\|^2$ with $\hat X=Y-f_\theta(Y)$; "choose $X_k$ typical of the images you will denoise"; "the conditional density is the narrow one"; residual $=$ skip connection $=$ negative noise $=\sigma^2\times$ score.`
];

})();
